      "name": "Zephyr SPDX builder"
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/atomic--test-atomic",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Passing proves that every atomic primitive (compare-and-set, arithmetic, load/store, bitwise, and single-bit operations on both scalar and pointer targets, plus the ATOMIC_DEFINE bit-array) performs the documented read-modify-write semantics: each call returns the prior value of the target and leaves the target updated to the expected result, including for negative operands and per-bit operations across the full atomic word width.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Invoke each atomic API in turn against a known target value.\n- For arithmetic operations, exercise both positive and negative operands.\n- For bit operations, iterate over every bit position of the atomic word.\n- For ATOMIC_DEFINE, set and clear each bit of a multi-word bit array.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Each call returns the target's previous value and the target holds the expected post-operation value.\n\n*See also:* atomic_cas(), atomic_add(), atomic_sub(), atomic_inc(), atomic_dec(), atomic_get(), atomic_set(), atomic_clear(), atomic_or(), atomic_and(), atomic_xor(), atomic_nand(), atomic_test_bit(), atomic_test_and_clear_bit(), atomic_test_and_set_bit(), atomic_clear_bit(), atomic_set_bit(), ATOMIC_DEFINE",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "atomic__test_atomic"
        }
      ],
      "name": "atomic__test_atomic",
      "summary": "Verify each atomic operation API mutates and returns the expected value."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/atomic--test-atomic-overflow",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The C standard leaves signed overflow undefined, but Zephyr's atomic_t must behave deterministically. Passing proves that incrementing an atomic_t past its maximum value produces the same wrapped result as an ordinary increment of an equivalently-typed scalar, both at the signed maximum and at the all-ones (unsigned wrap to zero) boundary. This guarantees an atomic variable can be reasoned about with plain arithmetic where it is logically safe to do so.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Seed an atomic_t and a matching scalar at the maximum signed value, increment both, and compare.\n- Seed both at the all-ones value (-1), increment both, and compare.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- After each increment the atomic value matches the plain-increment result and the expected wrapped boundary value.\n\n*See also:* atomic_inc()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "atomic__test_atomic_overflow"
        }
      ],
      "name": "atomic__test_atomic_overflow",
      "summary": "Verify atomic increment overflow wraps identically to plain increment."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/atomic--test-threads-access-atomic",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Passing proves that atomic_inc() is safe under preemptive concurrency: when multiple equal-priority preemptible threads increment a shared atomic_t while being time-sliced in and out, no increment is lost to a torn read-modify-write, so the final value equals the exact total number of increments performed.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Enable time slicing so equal-priority preemptible threads are scheduled fairly.\n- Start THREADS_NUM threads, each incrementing the shared atomic TEST_CYCLE times with a busy-wait between increments to force interleaving across slices.\n- Join all threads and disable time slicing.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The shared atomic equals TEST_CYCLE * THREADS_NUM.\n\n*See also:* atomic_inc()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "atomic__test_threads_access_atomic"
        }
      ],
      "name": "atomic__test_threads_access_atomic",
      "summary": "Verify atomic increments from concurrent threads are not lost."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/clock--test-clock-cycle-32",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Passing proves that the architecture's 32-bit hardware cycle counter (k_cycle_get_32()) is a monotonically increasing time source whose rate is consistent with the tick-derived uptime clock (k_uptime_get_32()): the number of cycles elapsed over one observed millisecond of uptime corresponds to at least one millisecond worth of cycles and at least one millisecond when converted to nanoseconds, cross-validating the cycle counter against uptime.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Align to a millisecond boundary and spin until the cycle counter advances, guarding against counter wrap-around.\n- Sample the cycle counter, align to a millisecond boundary, and spin until the 32-bit uptime advances by one millisecond.\n- Compute the cycle delta over that interval (skipping if the counter wrapped).\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The cycle delta exceeds one millisecond of cycles and, converted via k_cyc_to_ns_floor64(), exceeds one millisecond in nanoseconds.\n\n*See also:* k_cycle_get_32(), k_uptime_get_32()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "clock__test_clock_cycle_32"
        }
      ],
      "name": "clock__test_clock_cycle_32",
      "summary": "Verify the 32-bit cycle counter advances consistently with uptime."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/clock--test-clock-cycle-64",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Passing proves that, on platforms providing a 64-bit cycle counter, the 64-bit and 32-bit cycle counters measure the same elapsed time over a short sleep: the 64-bit delta is at least as large as the 32-bit delta (it does not lose resolution) yet remains below twice the 32-bit delta (it does not run at a different rate). The test skips on platforms without a 64-bit cycle counter.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Skip unless CONFIG_TIMER_HAS_64BIT_CYCLE_COUNTER is enabled.\n- Sample both the 64-bit and 32-bit cycle counters, sleep 1 ms, then sample again.\n- Compute the 32-bit and 64-bit deltas across the sleep.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The 64-bit delta is >= the 32-bit delta and < twice the 32-bit delta.\n\n*See also:* k_cycle_get_64()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "clock__test_clock_cycle_64"
        }
      ],
      "name": "clock__test_clock_cycle_64",
      "summary": "Verify the 64-bit cycle counter tracks the 32-bit counter over an interval."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/clock--test-clock-uptime",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Passing proves the system uptime clock is a forward-progressing time source usable from user mode: both the 64-bit and lower-32-bit millisecond uptime readings increase as wall time elapses and the 32-bit reading observes a millisecond boundary crossing.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Spin until k_uptime_get() advances by at least 5 ms.\n- Spin until k_uptime_get_32() advances by at least 5 ms.\n- Sample the 32-bit uptime, align to a millisecond boundary, and confirm it grew.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The millisecond uptime readings increase over time.\n\n*See also:* k_uptime_get(), k_uptime_get_32()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "clock__test_clock_uptime"
        }
      ],
      "name": "clock__test_clock_uptime",
      "summary": "Verify the uptime clock advances monotonically and reports deltas."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/clock--test-clock-uptime-delta",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Passing proves k_uptime_delta() measures the time elapsed since a caller-held reference time and updates the reference for the next measurement.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Seed a delta reference, then spin until k_uptime_delta() returns a non-zero elapsed time.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- k_uptime_delta() reports a non-zero interval once time has elapsed.\n\n*See also:* k_uptime_delta()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "clock__test_clock_uptime_delta"
        }
      ],
      "name": "clock__test_clock_uptime_delta",
      "summary": "Verify elapsed time reporting relative to a reference."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/clock--test-clock-uptime-seconds",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Passing proves k_uptime_seconds() reports the uptime in seconds consistently with the millisecond uptime clock: the value equals the floor of the millisecond reading converted to seconds.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Sample k_uptime_seconds() between two k_uptime_get() readings.\n- Check the seconds value against the floor of both millisecond readings.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The seconds reading is bounded by the floors of the surrounding millisecond readings.\n\n*See also:* k_uptime_seconds()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "clock__test_clock_uptime_seconds"
        }
      ],
      "name": "clock__test_clock_uptime_seconds",
      "summary": "Verify the system uptime is reported in whole seconds."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/clock--test-clock-uptime-ticks",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Passing proves k_uptime_ticks() is a forward-progressing reading of the system uptime in ticks, usable from user mode.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Sample k_uptime_ticks() and spin until it advances.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The tick reading increases as wall time elapses.\n\n*See also:* k_uptime_ticks()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "clock__test_clock_uptime_ticks"
        }
      ],
      "name": "clock__test_clock_uptime_ticks",
      "summary": "Verify the system uptime is reported in system ticks."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/clock--test-ms-time-duration",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Passing proves that k_timer_start() honors a millisecond expiry duration with correct timing semantics: the timer's expiry callback does not fire while less than the configured duration has elapsed, and fires exactly once once the full duration is exceeded, while the stop callback is not invoked on normal expiry.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Start the timer for DURATION ms one-shot and busy-wait LESS_DURATION ms; confirm neither the expiry nor stop counter has incremented.\n- Restart the timer, align to a tick, busy-wait just over DURATION ms, and confirm the expiry counter is exactly 1 and the stop counter is 0.\n- Stop the timer to clean up.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- No expiry before the duration elapses; exactly one expiry afterward and no stop-callback invocation.\n\n*See also:* k_timer_start(), k_timer_stop(), k_busy_wait()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "clock__test_ms_time_duration"
        }
      ],
      "name": "clock__test_ms_time_duration",
      "summary": "Verify a kernel timer expires only after its millisecond duration elapses."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/common-1cpu--test-nested-irq-offload",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Exercises the regression where irq_offload() is called from an already-running interrupt (a timer ISR) and the nested handler suspends the interrupted thread, forcing a context switch out of the nested interrupt. Passing proves nested interrupt offload runs the inner handler exactly once and returns control to the correct context without corruption. Skipped on SMP or when nested offload is off.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Raise the test thread priority and start a timer whose ISR calls irq_offload().\n- In a separate thread, spin asserting it is never resumed after the timer fires.\n- The timer ISR invokes the nested offload handler which suspends that thread.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Both the timer handler and the nested offload handler report having executed.\n\n*See also:* irq_offload()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "common_1cpu__test_nested_irq_offload"
        }
      ],
      "name": "common_1cpu__test_nested_irq_offload",
      "summary": "Verify a nested irq_offload() invoked from within an interrupt is handled safely."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/common-errno--test-thread-context",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Confirms that errno is thread-local: concurrent threads assigning distinct errno values, sleeping for different durations to force interleaving, must each still observe their own value when resumed, and the main thread's errno must be unaffected. Passing proves errno is saved and restored per thread on context switch.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Set the main thread's errno to a known value and record it.\n- Spawn N_THREADS threads, each assigning a unique errno then sleeping a staggered interval before re-reading and reporting its result via a FIFO.\n- Collect each thread's pass/fail result and re-check the main thread's errno.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Every thread observes its own errno value and the main thread's errno is unchanged.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_create()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "common_errno__test_thread_context"
        }
      ],
      "name": "common_errno__test_thread_context",
      "summary": "Verify each thread sees a private errno value preserved across context switches."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-broadcast-null",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A user-mode thread arms the ztest fault hook and calls k_condvar_broadcast() with a NULL pointer. The call must trigger a kernel fault; execution must not fall through to the ztest_test_fail() guard placed after it.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- A valid fault is taken at k_condvar_broadcast(NULL); the guard ztest_test_fail() in cond_broadcast_null() is never reached.\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_broadcast()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_broadcast_null"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_broadcast_null",
      "summary": "Verify k_condvar_broadcast(NULL) faults when called from user mode."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-init-null",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A user-mode thread arms the ztest fault hook and calls k_condvar_init() with a NULL pointer. The call must trigger a kernel fault; execution must not fall through to the ztest_test_fail() guard placed after it.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- A valid fault is taken at k_condvar_init(NULL); the guard ztest_test_fail() in cond_init_null() is never reached.\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_init()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_init_null"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_init_null",
      "summary": "Verify k_condvar_init(NULL) faults when called from user mode."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-multiple-threads-wait-wake",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "TOTAL_THREADS_WAITING (3) helper threads all block in k_condvar_wait() with K_FOREVER on the same condition variable. A waker thread then calls k_condvar_broadcast(). The test verifies broadcast reports exactly the number of threads it released and that all waiters return 0.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- k_condvar_broadcast() returns TOTAL_THREADS_WAITING.\n- Each k_condvar_wait() returns 0 (verified in condvar_wait_wake_task()).\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_broadcast()\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_wait()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_multiple_threads_wait_wake"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_multiple_threads_wait_wake",
      "summary": "Verify k_condvar_broadcast() wakes every thread on one condition variable."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-nowait-returns-eagain",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A helper thread calls k_condvar_wait() with a 0 tick timeout and returns -EAGAIN without blocking. Only afterwards does a second thread signal the condition variable. The test confirms the non-blocking wait result is unaffected by the later signal.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- k_condvar_wait() returns -EAGAIN (verified in condvar_wait_wake_task()).\n- k_condvar_signal() returns 0 with no waiter present.\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_wait()\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_signal()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_nowait_returns_eagain"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_nowait_returns_eagain",
      "summary": "Verify a zero-timeout wait returns -EAGAIN even when a signal follows."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-signal-null",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A user-mode thread arms the ztest fault hook and calls k_condvar_signal() with a NULL pointer. The call must trigger a kernel fault; execution must not fall through to the ztest_test_fail() guard placed after it.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- A valid fault is taken at k_condvar_signal(NULL); the guard ztest_test_fail() in cond_signal_null() is never reached.\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_signal()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_signal_null"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_signal_null",
      "summary": "Verify k_condvar_signal(NULL) faults when called from user mode."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-signal-wakes-highest-priority",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Three helper threads block on the same condition variable at distinct, lower priorities than the test thread. The test signals once per iteration and each woken thread appends its id to wake_order[]. The wakeups must follow priority order (highest priority first), not creation/FIFO order.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- wake_order[] is the waiter ids in descending-priority order.\n\n*Note:* Skipped when CONFIG_MP_MAX_NUM_CPUS > 1: with multiple CPUs waiters can run concurrently, so a strict wakeup order is not guaranteed.\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_signal()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_signal_wakes_highest_priority"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_signal_wakes_highest_priority",
      "summary": "Verify k_condvar_signal() wakes the highest-priority waiter first."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-signal-wakes-one",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "TOTAL_THREADS_WAITING (3) helper threads all block on the same condition variable with K_FOREVER. A single k_condvar_signal() must release exactly one of them; the others must stay blocked. This is the defining difference between signal and broadcast and was previously only covered for broadcast.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- k_condvar_signal() returns 0.\n- Exactly one waiter runs past k_condvar_wait() (woken_count == 1); the rest remain blocked until a later broadcast drains them.\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_signal()\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_broadcast()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_signal_wakes_one"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_signal_wakes_one",
      "summary": "Verify k_condvar_signal() releases exactly one of several waiters."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-static-define",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "static_condvar is created with K_CONDVAR_DEFINE() and never passed to k_condvar_init(). The test exercises signal/broadcast with no waiters and a full wait/signal round-trip to confirm the static initializer produces a usable object.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- k_condvar_signal()/k_condvar_broadcast() with no waiter return 0.\n- A blocked k_condvar_wait() is released by a signal and returns 0.\n\n*See also:* K_CONDVAR_DEFINE\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_wait()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_static_define"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_static_define",
      "summary": "Verify a statically defined condition variable works without init."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-usecase-broadcast",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Same producer/consumer scenario as test_condvar_usecase_signal, but the incrementer threads release the watcher with k_condvar_broadcast() instead of k_condvar_signal(). With a single watcher the observable outcome is identical; this exercises the broadcast wakeup path in the same use case.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Final count equals 145 (verified by zassert_equal() in _condvar_usecase()).\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_broadcast()\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_wait()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_usecase_broadcast"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_usecase_broadcast",
      "summary": "Verify a producer/consumer hand-off driven by k_condvar_broadcast()."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-usecase-signal",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "One watcher thread holds the mutex and waits on the condition variable until a shared counter reaches COUNT_LIMIT; two incrementer threads raise the counter under the same mutex and call k_condvar_signal() when the limit is hit. The deterministic final counter value proves the single-waiter wakeup fired and the mutex-protected shared state stayed consistent.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Final count equals 145: 20 increments from the two incrementer threads (2 x TCOUNT) plus 125 added by the watcher after it is released (verified by zassert_equal() in _condvar_usecase()).\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_signal()\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_wait()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_usecase_signal"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_usecase_signal",
      "summary": "Verify a producer/consumer hand-off driven by k_condvar_signal()."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-wait-forever",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A helper thread calls k_condvar_wait() with K_FOREVER and no thread ever signals the condition variable. The waiter must remain blocked. The test confirms this explicitly by joining the helper with a finite deadline: the join must time out, proving the thread is still alive and blocked. The waiter is then aborted.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Start a helper thread that calls k_condvar_wait(..., K_FOREVER).\n- k_thread_join() the helper with a finite deadline.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- k_thread_join() returns -EAGAIN (the deadline expires while the helper is still blocked). A return of 0 would mean a spurious wakeup let the helper terminate, which condvar_wait_task() also catches with zassert_unreachable().\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_wait()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_join()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_forever"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_forever",
      "summary": "Verify a K_FOREVER waiter stays blocked when no signal is sent."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-wait-forever-wake",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A helper thread locks a mutex and calls k_condvar_wait() with a K_FOREVER timeout, so it blocks indefinitely. A second thread then calls k_condvar_signal() on the same condition variable. The test verifies that the signal wakes the blocked waiter, which returns 0 (woken, not timed out).\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Initialize the condition variable.\n- Start a waiter thread that calls k_condvar_wait(..., K_FOREVER).\n- Start a waker thread that calls k_condvar_signal().\n\nExpected result:\n\n- k_condvar_wait() in the waiter returns 0 (verified in condvar_wait_wake_task()).\n- k_condvar_signal() returns 0.\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_wait()\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_signal()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_forever_wake"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_forever_wake",
      "summary": "Verify a thread blocked forever is released by k_condvar_signal()."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-wait-forever-wake-from-isr",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A helper thread blocks in k_condvar_wait() with K_FOREVER. The test then issues k_condvar_signal() from an ISR via irq_offload(). The test verifies the signal raised in interrupt context releases the waiter, which returns 0.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- k_condvar_wait() returns 0 (verified in condvar_wait_wake_task()).\n\n*Note:* This is a kernel-mode test (ZTEST, not ZTEST_USER) because signalling is performed from an ISR.\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_wait()\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_signal()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_forever_wake_from_isr"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_forever_wake_from_isr",
      "summary": "Verify a thread blocked forever is woken by a signal from ISR context."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-wait-nowait",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A helper thread calls k_condvar_wait() with a timeout of 0 ticks. With no signal pending, the call must not block and must return -EAGAIN immediately.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- k_condvar_wait() returns -EAGAIN (verified in condvar_wait_task()).\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_wait()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_nowait"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_nowait",
      "summary": "Verify k_condvar_wait() with a zero timeout returns -EAGAIN immediately."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-wait-nowait-keeps-mutex",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "k_condvar_wait() with K_NO_WAIT returns -EAGAIN without touching the mutex (a distinct code path from the timeout path). This test locks a mutex, makes the no-wait call, then unlocks to prove the caller still owns it.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- k_condvar_wait() returns -EAGAIN.\n- k_mutex_unlock() succeeds (returns -EPERM if ownership was lost).\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_wait()\n\n*See also:* k_mutex_unlock()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_nowait_keeps_mutex"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_nowait_keeps_mutex",
      "summary": "Verify the K_NO_WAIT path of k_condvar_wait() leaves the mutex held."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-wait-null",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A user-mode thread arms the ztest fault hook and calls k_condvar_wait() with NULL condition variable and mutex pointers. The call must trigger a kernel fault; execution must not fall through to the ztest_test_fail() guard placed after it.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- A valid fault is taken at k_condvar_wait(NULL, NULL, ...); the guard ztest_test_fail() in cond_wait_null() is never reached.\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_wait()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_null"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_null",
      "summary": "Verify k_condvar_wait(NULL, NULL, ...) faults when called from user mode."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-wait-recursive-mutex-not-released",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Unlike POSIX pthread_cond_wait(), which releases a recursively locked mutex completely and restores the count on return, k_condvar_wait() calls k_mutex_unlock() exactly once. When the mutex is locked recursively the count is only decremented, so the waiting thread keeps ownership across the wait.\n\nThe test locks a mutex twice, then waits on a condition variable. While it is blocked, a contender thread tries to acquire the mutex with a short timeout and must fail, proving the mutex was not released. A separate thread signals the wait afterwards so the test can unwind cleanly.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The contender's k_mutex_lock() times out with -EAGAIN (current behaviour).\n- k_condvar_wait() returns 0 once signaled and both recursive levels unlock.\n\n*Note:* KNOWN LIMITATION / divergence from POSIX. If kernel/condvar.c is changed to fully release a recursively locked mutex, this expectation must be updated (the contender would then succeed).\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_wait()\n\n*See also:* k_mutex_lock()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_recursive_mutex_not_released"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_recursive_mutex_not_released",
      "summary": "Document that k_condvar_wait() does not fully release a recursive mutex."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-wait-signaled-keeps-mutex",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Complements the timeout-relock test for the success path: a waker thread signals the condition variable while the test thread is blocked in k_condvar_wait(). On return the mutex must be re-acquired by the caller.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- k_condvar_wait() returns 0 (signaled).\n- k_mutex_unlock() succeeds (returns -EPERM if the mutex was not re-locked).\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_wait()\n\n*See also:* k_mutex_unlock()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_signaled_keeps_mutex"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_signaled_keeps_mutex",
      "summary": "Verify a signaled k_condvar_wait() returns with the mutex held."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-wait-timeout",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A helper thread calls k_condvar_wait() with a 50 ms timeout and no thread ever signals the condition variable. The test verifies the wait fails with -EAGAIN once the timeout elapses.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- k_condvar_wait() returns -EAGAIN (verified in condvar_wait_task()).\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_wait()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_timeout"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_timeout",
      "summary": "Verify k_condvar_wait() returns -EAGAIN when its timeout expires."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-wait-timeout-relocks-mutex",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Per the contract documented in kernel/condvar.c, k_condvar_wait() must always return with the mutex held by the calling thread, even when the wait times out (matching POSIX semantics for pthread_cond_timedwait). The test locks a mutex, waits with a short timeout and no waker so the wait expires, then unlocks the mutex to prove ownership was restored.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- k_condvar_wait() returns -EAGAIN on timeout.\n- k_mutex_unlock() succeeds; a regression that failed to re-lock the mutex would make it return -EPERM (mutex not locked by the calling thread).\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_wait()\n\n*See also:* k_mutex_unlock()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_timeout_relocks_mutex"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_timeout_relocks_mutex",
      "summary": "Verify k_condvar_wait() re-locks the mutex when the wait times out."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-wake-before-timeout",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A helper thread calls k_condvar_wait() with a 100 ms timeout and a second thread signals the condition variable immediately. The signal arrives before the timeout expires, so the waiter must return 0 (woken), not -EAGAIN.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- k_condvar_wait() returns 0 (verified in condvar_wait_wake_task()).\n- k_condvar_signal() returns 0.\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_wait()\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_signal()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wake_before_timeout"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wake_before_timeout",
      "summary": "Verify a finite-timeout waiter is woken by a signal before it times out."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-wake-no-waiters",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Exercises the no-waiter branches: with an empty wait queue, signal and broadcast must be no-ops that report zero woken threads.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- k_condvar_signal() returns 0.\n- k_condvar_broadcast() returns 0.\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_signal()\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_broadcast()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wake_no_waiters"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wake_no_waiters",
      "summary": "Verify k_condvar_signal()/k_condvar_broadcast() with no waiters return 0."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-multiple-condvar-wait-wake",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "TOTAL_THREADS_WAITING (3) helper threads each block on a distinct condition variable (multiple_condvar[i]). A matching set of waker threads each wakes its own condition variable. With one waiter per condition variable, each wake must release exactly one thread, demonstrating that condition variables are independent and do not cross-signal.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Each wake call releases exactly one waiter (returns 1).\n- Each k_condvar_wait() returns 0 (verified in condvar_multiple_wait_wake_task()).\n\n*Note:* Easily confused with test_condvar_multiple_threads_wait_wake, which has many waiters on a SINGLE condition variable. This one is the \"one condition variable per waiter\" case. Because woken is 1, condvar_multiple_wake_task() always takes its k_condvar_broadcast() branch, so its k_condvar_signal() path is not exercised here.\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_signal()\n\n*See also:* k_condvar_broadcast()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_multiple_condvar_wait_wake"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_multiple_condvar_wait_wake",
      "summary": "Verify independent condition variables each wake their own single waiter."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/constructor--test-constructor",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Passing proves that functions marked with the GCC constructor attribute are invoked during system startup before the test runs, and that prioritized constructors execute in ascending priority order ahead of unprioritized ones.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Register three constructors that each record an identifier on invocation: priority 101, priority 1000, and one with no explicit priority.\n- In the test body, inspect the recorded count and ordering.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- All three constructors ran (count is 3), priority 101 ran first, priority 1000 ran second, and the unprioritized constructor ran last.\n\n*See also:* ZTEST_SUITE()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "constructor__test_constructor"
        }
      ],
      "name": "constructor__test_constructor",
      "summary": "Verify C constructors run before main in priority order."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/context-cpu-idle--test-cpu-idle",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The architecture provides an idle primitive that suspends the CPU when there is no work to do and resumes on the next interrupt. The test enters k_cpu_idle() and confirms the CPU stayed idle for the expected duration before an interrupt woke it.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Record the system time before idling.\n- Enter k_cpu_idle() and wait for an interrupt to resume execution.\n- On wake-up, record the system time again and compare the elapsed time.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The CPU idles and resumes after the expected time has elapsed.\n\n*See also:* k_cpu_idle()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "context_cpu_idle__test_cpu_idle"
        }
      ],
      "name": "context_cpu_idle__test_cpu_idle",
      "summary": "Verify k_cpu_idle() idles the CPU until an interrupt wakes it."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/context-cpu-idle--test-cpu-idle-atomic",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The architecture provides an atomic idle primitive that suspends the CPU with interrupts locked and resumes when an interrupt arrives, without losing a wake-up that races the idle. The test arms a timer, enters k_cpu_atomic_idle(), and confirms the CPU stayed idle for the expected duration and was woken by the timer.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Record the system time before idling.\n- Lock interrupts, arm a timer, and enter k_cpu_atomic_idle().\n- On wake-up, record the system time again and compare against the timer.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The CPU idles until the timer fires and the elapsed time matches the timer duration within tolerance.\n\n*See also:* k_cpu_atomic_idle()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "context_cpu_idle__test_cpu_idle_atomic"
        }
      ],
      "name": "context_cpu_idle__test_cpu_idle_atomic",
      "summary": "Verify k_cpu_atomic_idle() idles the CPU until an interrupt wakes it."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
