        }
      ],
      "name": "mutex_complex__test_mutex",
      "summary": "Main thread to test thread_mutex_xxx interfaces."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/mutex-complex--test-mutex-multithread-competition",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Define three threads, and set a higher priority for two of them, and set a lower priority for the last one. Then Add a delay between creating the two high priority threads. Test point:\n\n- Any number of threads may wait on a mutex locked by others simultaneously.\n- When the mutex is released, it is took by the highest priority thread that has waited longest.\n\n*See also:* sys_mutex_lock()\n\n*See also:* sys_mutex_unlock()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "mutex_complex__test_mutex_multithread_competition"
        }
      ],
      "name": "mutex_complex__test_mutex_multithread_competition",
      "summary": "Test multi-threads to take mutex."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/mutex-complex--test-user-access",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A user thread cannot operate on a sys_mutex located outside the memory domain it has been granted access to.\n\n*See also:* sys_mutex_lock()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "mutex_complex__test_user_access"
        }
      ],
      "name": "mutex_complex__test_user_access",
      "summary": "Verify user-mode access control for sys_mutex objects."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/no-multithreading--test-no-multithreading-busy-wait",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "With CONFIG_MULTITHREADING=n there is no scheduler and no other thread to switch to, so a busy wait has to be served entirely by the caller spinning while the system clock keeps advancing. Uptime is sampled around a busy wait of a known length to confirm both that the clock runs and that the delay is of the requested duration.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Spin until the uptime changes, so the measurement starts on a tick boundary, failing if it never does.\n- Sample the uptime, call k_busy_wait() for 10 ms and sample it again.\n- Compare the elapsed uptime against the requested delay.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The uptime advances, and the elapsed time is within 2 ms of the 10 ms that were requested.\n\n*See also:* k_busy_wait()\n\n*See also:* k_uptime_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "no_multithreading__test_no_multithreading_busy_wait"
        }
      ],
      "name": "no_multithreading__test_no_multithreading_busy_wait",
      "summary": "Verify that k_busy_wait() delays for the requested time when multithreading is disabled."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/no-multithreading--test-no-multithreading-cpu-idle",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Without multithreading there is no idle thread, so the application itself calls k_cpu_idle() to wait. The CPU must suspend until an interrupt arrives and must resume with the timer's expiry handler having run, which is how a single-threaded application waits for time to pass without spinning.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Start a 10 ms timer whose handler raises a flag, sampling the uptime.\n- Call k_cpu_idle(); on a tickful kernel, where every tick wakes the CPU, idle again until the handler has actually run.\n- Check the flag and measure the elapsed uptime.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The CPU resumes with the timer handler having run.\n- At least the requested 10 ms elapsed, and no more than one tick plus a small measurement margin beyond it.\n\n*See also:* k_cpu_idle()\n\n*See also:* k_timer_start()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "no_multithreading__test_no_multithreading_cpu_idle"
        }
      ],
      "name": "no_multithreading__test_no_multithreading_cpu_idle",
      "summary": "Verify that k_cpu_idle() sleeps until a timer interrupt wakes it."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/no-multithreading--test-no-multithreading-irq-lock",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Interrupt locking is the only mutual exclusion mechanism left once multithreading is disabled, so it has to hold off the timer ISR: a timer that expires while interrupts are locked must not run its handler until they are unlocked. The handler sets a flag through the timer user data, which is sampled on both sides of the unlock.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Start a 10 ms timer whose handler raises a flag.\n- Lock interrupts, busy-wait past the timer deadline with a tick of slack and check the flag is still clear.\n- Unlock interrupts, allowing the pending timer interrupt to be delivered.\n- On a tickful kernel, busy-wait one more tick so a second announce reaches the timer deadline, then check the flag.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The handler does not run while interrupts are locked.\n- The handler runs once interrupts are unlocked.\n\n*See also:* irq_lock()\n\n*See also:* irq_unlock()\n\n*See also:* k_timer_start()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "no_multithreading__test_no_multithreading_irq_lock"
        }
      ],
      "name": "no_multithreading__test_no_multithreading_irq_lock",
      "summary": "Verify that locking interrupts defers a timer expiry handler."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/no-multithreading--test-no-multithreading-sys-init",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The initialization levels are a property of the boot sequence rather than of the scheduler, so disabling multithreading must not change them. Each registered init function checks that it is entered at its own position in the sequence and then advances a counter, so a wrong order is recorded as it happens rather than being inferred afterwards.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Register one init function at PRE_KERNEL_1, PRE_KERNEL_2 and POST_KERNEL, each asserting its position and incrementing a shared counter.\n- After boot, read the counter.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- All three init functions ran, in level order, leaving the counter at 3.\n\n*See also:* SYS_INIT()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "no_multithreading__test_no_multithreading_sys_init"
        }
      ],
      "name": "no_multithreading__test_no_multithreading_sys_init",
      "summary": "Verify that SYS_INIT functions run in order without multithreading."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/obj-core--test-obj-core-condvar",
