      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "poll_api_1cpu__test_poll_zero_events"
        }
      ],
      "name": "poll_api_1cpu__test_poll_zero_events",
      "summary": "k_poll() with zero events returns -EAGAIN"
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/protection--test-protection-exec-data",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Writable data must be mapped without execute permission, so code copied into a data buffer cannot be run. Skipped on configurations without data execution prevention, where the hardware cannot enforce this.\n\nThe suite installs a fatal error handler that reports the case as passed and moves on, so a caught fault is the success path: reaching the zassert_unreachable() after the access means the region was not protected.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Copy a small function into a buffer in .data.\n- Call into that buffer.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The call raises a fatal error; the code after it is never reached.",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "protection__test_protection_exec_data"
        }
      ],
      "name": "protection__test_protection_exec_data",
      "summary": "Verify that executing from .data faults."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/protection--test-protection-exec-heap",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Heap memory must be mapped without execute permission, for the same reason as the stack and data cases. Skipped when there is no heap configured or the platform has no data execution prevention.\n\nThe suite installs a fatal error handler that reports the case as passed and moves on, so a caught fault is the success path: reaching the zassert_unreachable() after the access means the region was not protected.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Allocate a buffer with k_malloc() and copy a small function into it.\n- Call into that buffer.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The call raises a fatal error; the code after it is never reached.\n\n*See also:* k_malloc()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "protection__test_protection_exec_heap"
        }
      ],
      "name": "protection__test_protection_exec_heap",
      "summary": "Verify that executing from the heap faults."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/protection--test-protection-exec-stack",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A thread stack must be mapped without execute permission, which is what stops a stack overflow from being turned into code execution. Skipped on configurations without data execution prevention.\n\nThe suite installs a fatal error handler that reports the case as passed and moves on, so a caught fault is the success path: reaching the zassert_unreachable() after the access means the region was not protected.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Copy a small function into a buffer on the current thread's stack.\n- Call into that buffer.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The call raises a fatal error; the code after it is never reached.",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "protection__test_protection_exec_stack"
        }
      ],
      "name": "protection__test_protection_exec_stack",
      "summary": "Verify that executing from the stack faults."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/protection--test-protection-write-rodata",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Read-only data must be mapped without write permission, so an attempt to modify it has to trap rather than silently succeed.\n\nThe suite installs a fatal error handler that reports the case as passed and moves on, so a caught fault is the success path: reaching the zassert_unreachable() after the access means the region was not protected.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Take a pointer to a variable in .rodata.\n- Write the inverse of its value through that pointer.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The write raises a fatal error; the code after it is never reached.",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "protection__test_protection_write_rodata"
        }
      ],
      "name": "protection__test_protection_write_rodata",
      "summary": "Verify that writing to .rodata faults."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/protection--test-protection-write-text",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Executable code must be mapped without write permission, otherwise running code could be rewritten in place. The test copies one function over another, which is the shape a real code-injection attempt would take.\n\nThe suite installs a fatal error handler that reports the case as passed and moves on, so a caught fault is the success path: reaching the zassert_unreachable() after the access means the region was not protected.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Copy the body of one function over another function in .text.\n- Call the overwritten function.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The write raises a fatal error; the code after it is never reached.",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "protection__test_protection_write_text"
        }
      ],
      "name": "protection__test_protection_write_text",
      "summary": "Verify that writing to .text faults."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api--test-access-kernel-obj-with-priv-data",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The k_queue APIs keep bookkeeping data inside structures visible from user mode, so direct access must be gated by the kernel object permission system. A user thread without permission on the queue object that calls a k_queue API must trigger a kernel oops rather than read private kernel data.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Initialize a queue and insert an item from supervisor mode.\n- Spawn a user thread (with no granted access) that calls k_queue_is_empty().\n- Mark the fault as expected and join the thread.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The unprivileged access triggers the expected kernel oops.\n\n*See also:* k_queue_is_empty()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api__test_access_kernel_obj_with_priv_data"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api__test_access_kernel_obj_with_priv_data",
      "summary": "Verify a user thread faults accessing a queue it has no permission on."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api--test-auto-free",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Elements added with the alloc APIs and the kernel objects allocated by the previous user-mode test must be released automatically (queue elements when dequeued, objects when permitted threads exit). The test confirms the whole pool is available again by reallocating all of it.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- After the preceding alloc-heavy test, allocate the entire pool in chunks.\n- Verify every allocation succeeds, then free them.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- All pool memory is reclaimable, proving prior resources were auto-freed.\n\n*See also:* k_queue_alloc_append()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api__test_auto_free"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api__test_auto_free",
      "summary": "Verify allocated queue resources are auto-freed back to the pool."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api--test-multiple-queues",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api__test_multiple_queues"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api__test_multiple_queues",
      "summary": "Operate on multiple independent queues."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api--test-queue-alloc",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "k_queue_alloc_append()/k_queue_alloc_prepend() allocate a container from the calling thread's heap. With no pool (or a too-small pool) the insertion must fail and leave the queue empty; with a sufficient pool it must succeed and the item becomes retrievable.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- With no resource pool assigned, attempt an alloc append; verify it fails.\n- Assign a too-small pool, attempt an alloc prepend; verify it fails and the queue stays empty.\n- Assign a sufficient pool, alloc prepend succeeds and the item can be gotten.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Allocation fails without enough pool memory and succeeds with it.\n\n*See also:* k_queue_alloc_append()\n\n*See also:* k_queue_alloc_prepend()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_heap_assign()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api__test_queue_alloc"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api__test_queue_alloc",
      "summary": "Verify alloc append/prepend honor the thread's resource pool."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api--test-queue-alloc-append-null",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Verify that the parameter of the API is NULL, what will happen.\n\n*See also:* k_queue_alloc_append()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api__test_queue_alloc_append_null"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api__test_queue_alloc_append_null",
      "summary": "Test k_queue_alloc_append() failure scenario."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api--test-queue-alloc-append-user",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Appending items adds each to the tail, so reading the queue back returns them in insertion (FIFO) order. Exercised from a user-mode thread on an allocated queue object.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Alloc-append a sequence of items 0..N-1 from a user thread.\n- Get all items and verify they come out in insertion order (0..N-1).\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Items are returned oldest-first (FIFO).\n\n*See also:* k_queue_alloc_append()\n\n*See also:* k_queue_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api__test_queue_alloc_append_user"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api__test_queue_alloc_append_user",
      "summary": "Verify k_queue_alloc_append() yields FIFO order from user mode."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api--test-queue-alloc-prepend-null",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Verify that the parameter of the API is NULL, what will happen.\n\n*See also:* k_queue_alloc_prepend()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api__test_queue_alloc_prepend_null"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api__test_queue_alloc_prepend_null",
      "summary": "Test k_queue_alloc_prepend() failure scenario."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api--test-queue-alloc-prepend-user",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Prepending items pushes each to the front, so reading the queue back returns them in reverse insertion (LIFO) order. Exercised from a user-mode thread on an allocated queue object.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Alloc-prepend a sequence of items 0..N-1 from a user thread.\n- Get all items and verify they come out in reverse order (N-1..0).\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Items are returned newest-first (LIFO).\n\n*See also:* k_queue_alloc_prepend()\n\n*See also:* k_queue_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api__test_queue_alloc_prepend_user"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api__test_queue_alloc_prepend_user",
      "summary": "Verify k_queue_alloc_prepend() yields LIFO order from user mode."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api--test-queue-append-list-error",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "According to the API k_queue_append_list to design some error condition to verify error branch of the API.\n\n- Verify that the list's head is empty.\n- Verify that the list's tail is empty.\n- Verify that append list to the queue when a sub-thread is waiting for data.\n\n*See also:* k_queue_append_list()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api__test_queue_append_list_error"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api__test_queue_append_list_error",
      "summary": "Test k_queue_append_list() failure scenario."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api--test-queue-append-list-order",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "k_queue_append_list() must transfer a caller-built singly-linked list to the back of the queue with its order preserved.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Append one item so the queue is non-empty.\n- Link the items of an array into a list.\n- Append the list and confirm it lands behind the pre-existing item.\n- Get all items and verify list order is preserved.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The pre-existing item is delivered first, then every list item in its original order.\n\n*See also:* k_queue_append_list()\n\n*See also:* k_queue_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api__test_queue_append_list_order"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api__test_queue_append_list_order",
      "summary": "Verify appending a singly-linked item list to the back of a queue."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api--test-queue-cancel-wait-error",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Verify that cancelling the wait on a queue causes a blocked k_queue_get() to return without an item.\n\n*See also:* k_queue_cancel_wait()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api__test_queue_cancel_wait_error"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api__test_queue_cancel_wait_error",
      "summary": "Test k_queue_cancel_wait() error scenario."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api--test-queue-define",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A queue created with K_QUEUE_DEFINE() must be fully initialized at boot: empty and immediately usable for data passing without any run-time initialization call.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Check the statically defined queue is empty.\n- Append an item and get it back, verifying the same item is returned.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The statically defined queue accepts and delivers items as-is.\n\n*See also:* K_QUEUE_DEFINE",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api__test_queue_define"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api__test_queue_define",
      "summary": "Verify a queue defined at compile time is ready for use."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api--test-queue-get-null",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Verify that the parameter of the API is NULL, what will happen.\n\n*See also:* k_queue_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api__test_queue_get_null"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api__test_queue_get_null",
      "summary": "Test k_queue_get() failure scenario."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api--test-queue-init",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "k_queue_init() must yield an empty queue that is immediately usable for data passing.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Initialize a queue at run time and check it is empty.\n- Append an item and get it back, verifying the same item is returned.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The initialized queue is empty and accepts and delivers items.\n\n*See also:* k_queue_init()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api__test_queue_init"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api__test_queue_init",
      "summary": "Verify run-time initialization of a queue."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api--test-queue-init-null",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Verify that the parameter of API k_queue_init() is NULL, what will happen.\n\n*See also:* k_queue_init()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api__test_queue_init_null"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api__test_queue_init_null",
      "summary": "Test k_queue_init() failure scenario."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api--test-queue-insert-after",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "k_queue_insert() adds an item immediately after a caller-specified node already in the queue, so dequeuing must return the inserted item between its predecessor and the items that followed it.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Append two items.\n- Insert a third item after the first one.\n- Get all items and verify the inserted item comes out second.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The inserted item is dequeued directly after the node it was inserted behind.\n\n*See also:* k_queue_insert()\n\n*See also:* k_queue_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api__test_queue_insert_after"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api__test_queue_insert_after",
      "summary": "Verify k_queue_insert() places an item after a given node."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api--test-queue-is-empty-null",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Verify that the parameter of the API is NULL, what will happen.\n\n*See also:* k_queue_is_empty()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api__test_queue_is_empty_null"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api__test_queue_is_empty_null",
      "summary": "Test k_queue_is_empty() failure scenario."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api--test-queue-isr2thread",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Items are enqueued in thread context and dequeued from interrupt context (via irq_offload()), confirming k_queue_get() is ISR-safe and the ISR receives every item. Run against both an init()ed and a DEFINE()d queue.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- In thread context, insert items into the queue.\n- From an ISR, get all items and verify their addresses/order.\n- Repeat for a statically defined queue.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The ISR dequeues every thread-enqueued item in the expected order.\n\n*See also:* k_queue_append()\n\n*See also:* k_queue_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api__test_queue_isr2thread"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api__test_queue_isr2thread",
      "summary": "Verify data passes from a thread to an ISR through a queue."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api--test-queue-merge-list-error",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Verify the API k_queue_merge_slist when a slist is empty or a slist's tail is null.\n\n*See also:* k_queue_merge_slist()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api__test_queue_merge_list_error"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api__test_queue_merge_list_error",
      "summary": "Test k_queue_merge_slist() failure scenario."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api--test-queue-merge-slist-order",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "k_queue_merge_slist() must move every node of a sys_slist to the back of the queue in list order and leave the source list empty.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Append one item so the queue is non-empty.\n- Build a sys_slist with two nodes and merge it into the queue.\n- Verify the source list is empty after the merge.\n- Get all items and confirm the merged nodes land behind the pre-existing item, in list order.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The pre-existing item is delivered first, then every merged node in order, and the source list is emptied.\n\n*See also:* k_queue_merge_slist()\n\n*See also:* k_queue_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api__test_queue_merge_slist_order"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api__test_queue_merge_slist_order",
      "summary": "Verify merging a sys_slist empties the list into the queue."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api--test-queue-peek-head-null",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Verify that the parameter of the API is NULL, what will happen.\n\n*See also:* k_queue_peek_head()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api__test_queue_peek_head_null"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api__test_queue_peek_head_null",
      "summary": "Test k_queue_peek_head() failure scenario."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api--test-queue-peek-tail-null",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Verify that the parameter of the API is NULL, what will happen.\n\n*See also:* k_queue_peek_tail()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api__test_queue_peek_tail_null"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api__test_queue_peek_tail_null",
      "summary": "Test k_queue_peek_tail() failure scenario."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api--test-queue-prepend-order",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "k_queue_prepend() adds an item to the front of the queue, so items prepended after an append must be dequeued first, with the most recently prepended item coming out first.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Add one item, then prepend two items.\n- Get all three items and verify the order: most recently prepended first, appended item last.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Items are dequeued in prepend-reverse order followed by the appended item.\n\n*See also:* k_queue_prepend()\n\n*See also:* k_queue_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api__test_queue_prepend_order"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api__test_queue_prepend_order",
      "summary": "Verify prepended items are dequeued before appended ones."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api--test-queue-thread2isr",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Items are enqueued from interrupt context (via irq_offload()) and dequeued in thread context, confirming the queue insertion APIs are ISR-safe and the thread receives every item. Run against both an init()ed and a DEFINE()d queue.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- From an ISR, insert items into the queue.\n- In thread context, get all items and verify their addresses/order.\n- Repeat for a statically defined queue.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The thread dequeues every ISR-enqueued item in the expected order.\n\n*See also:* k_queue_append()\n\n*See also:* k_queue_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api__test_queue_thread2isr"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api__test_queue_thread2isr",
      "summary": "Verify data passes from an ISR to a thread through a queue."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api--test-queue-unique-append",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "k_queue_unique_append() must add an item only if it is not already queued: a first append of an item succeeds, a second append of the same item fails, and appending a different item succeeds.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Append an item and verify it succeeds.\n- Append the same item again and verify it fails.\n- Append a different item and verify it succeeds.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Duplicate appends return false; unique appends return true.\n\n*See also:* k_queue_unique_append()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api__test_queue_unique_append"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api__test_queue_unique_append",
      "summary": "Verify k_queue_unique_append() rejects duplicate entries."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api-1cpu--test-queue-get-2threads",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "When multiple threads block on an empty queue with K_FOREVER, each appended item must wake exactly one waiter so that every blocked getter eventually receives data.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Start two threads that each block in k_queue_get(K_FOREVER).\n- Append two items to the queue.\n- Confirm both threads complete (each got one item).\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Both waiting threads are woken, each receiving one item.\n\n*See also:* k_queue_get()\n\n*See also:* k_queue_append()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api_1cpu__test_queue_get_2threads"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api_1cpu__test_queue_get_2threads",
      "summary": "Verify two threads blocked on a queue are each woken by an append."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api-1cpu--test-queue-get-fail",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "With no data queued, k_queue_get() must return NULL rather than block forever or return stale data. Checked both with K_NO_WAIT (immediate) and with a finite timeout that is allowed to expire.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Initialize an empty queue.\n- Call k_queue_get() with K_NO_WAIT and verify it returns NULL.\n- Call k_queue_get() with a finite timeout and verify it returns NULL.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Both k_queue_get() calls return NULL.\n\n*See also:* k_queue_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api_1cpu__test_queue_get_fail"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api_1cpu__test_queue_get_fail",
      "summary": "Verify k_queue_get() on an empty queue returns NULL."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api-1cpu--test-queue-loop",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Stresses the queue by cycling append/prepend/get/remove operations through main-thread, ISR and spawned-thread contexts many times. Across every iteration the items must be passed and removed without loss, duplication or reordering.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- For LOOPS iterations: append/prepend from the main thread, find-and-remove plus get/append from an ISR and a spawned thread, then drain.\n- Verify each get returns the expected item and each remove succeeds.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Data passes correctly across all contexts on every iteration.\n\n*See also:* k_queue_append()\n\n*See also:* k_queue_get()\n\n*See also:* k_queue_remove()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api_1cpu__test_queue_loop"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api_1cpu__test_queue_loop",
      "summary": "Verify queue data integrity under repeated cross-context traffic."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api-1cpu--test-queue-multithread-competition",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Any number of threads may block on an empty queue. When data arrives it must go to the highest-priority waiter, and among equal-priority waiters to the one that has waited longest. Three threads (one low priority, two equal higher priority created with a delay between them) block, then three items are appended; each thread must receive the item matching its expected order.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Start a low-priority waiter and two higher-priority waiters (staggered).\n- Append three distinct items.\n- Each thread asserts it received the item matching its priority/wait rank.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The longest-waiting highest-priority thread gets the first item, the other high-priority thread next, and the low-priority thread last.\n\n*See also:* k_queue_get()\n\n*See also:* k_queue_append()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api_1cpu__test_queue_multithread_competition"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api_1cpu__test_queue_multithread_competition",
      "summary": "Verify queued data is delivered by priority then wait time."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api-1cpu--test-queue-poll-race",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Guards against a historical CONFIG_POLL race: an insert could wake a lower-priority waiter, then a higher-priority waiter could steal the item before the first ran, causing the lower-priority getter to spuriously return NULL before its timeout. Two consumers of different priority block on the queue; appending two items must deliver exactly one to each (two total) with no spurious early return.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Start two consumer threads of different priority that block on the queue.\n- Append two items.\n- Verify neither consumer woke prematurely and exactly two gets succeeded.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The two appended items are consumed (low_count + mid_count == 2) with no spurious NULL return.\n\n*See also:* k_queue_append()\n\n*See also:* k_queue_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api_1cpu__test_queue_poll_race"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api_1cpu__test_queue_poll_race",
      "summary": "Verify an append wakes exactly one waiter (CONFIG_POLL race)."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api-1cpu--test-queue-supv-to-user",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A supervisor thread fills a queue with both plain and alloc-appended elements, which a user-mode child thread must read back in order (with correct head/tail peeks). The child then blocks on an empty queue and must be released by k_queue_cancel_wait() returning NULL.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Supervisor appends interleaved plain and alloc-appended items.\n- Start a user child that peeks head/tail, gets all items in order, then blocks on an empty get.\n- Call k_queue_cancel_wait() and verify the child's blocked get returns NULL.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The user thread reads every item in order and the cancel releases its wait.\n\n*See also:* k_queue_alloc_append()\n\n*See also:* k_queue_get()\n\n*See also:* k_queue_cancel_wait()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api_1cpu__test_queue_supv_to_user"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api_1cpu__test_queue_supv_to_user",
      "summary": "Verify supervisor-queued data reaches a user thread and cancel wait."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/queue-api-1cpu--test-queue-thread2thread",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A consumer thread blocks on k_queue_get() while the main thread enqueues items using every insertion API (insert, append, prepend, append_list, merge_slist). The consumer must receive all items in the resulting dequeue order. Run against both a k_queue_init()ed and a K_QUEUE_DEFINE()d queue.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Start a consumer thread that drains the queue.\n- Insert items via insert/append/prepend/append_list/merge_slist.\n- Synchronize on a semaphore once the consumer has read all items.\n- Repeat for a statically defined queue.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The consumer dequeues every item, matching the expected ordering.\n\n*See also:* k_queue_append()\n\n*See also:* k_queue_prepend()\n\n*See also:* k_queue_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "queue_api_1cpu__test_queue_thread2thread"
        }
      ],
      "name": "queue_api_1cpu__test_queue_thread2thread",
      "summary": "Verify data passes between two threads through a queue in order."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/runtime-stack-safety--test-stack-safety-full-check",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A full stack safety check scans the whole stack to compute the exact unused space, reports it through the output pointer, and invokes the handler only when that unused space has dropped below the configured threshold. When it does fire, the handler must receive the checked thread, the reported unused amount and the caller-supplied argument.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- With the threshold disabled (0), run a check and confirm the handler does not fire while a plausible unused value is still reported.\n- Set the threshold just above the reported unused space, run a check, and confirm the handler fires once with the matching thread, unused amount and argument.\n- Set the threshold below the unused space, run a check, and confirm the handler does not fire.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The unused space is reported on every successful check.\n- The handler fires exactly when unused space is below the threshold and is passed the correct thread, unused amount and argument.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_runtime_stack_safety_full_check()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "runtime_stack_safety__test_stack_safety_full_check"
        }
      ],
      "name": "runtime_stack_safety__test_stack_safety_full_check",
      "summary": "Verify the full check reports unused space and fires only below threshold."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/runtime-stack-safety--test-stack-safety-full-check-null-handler",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The handler argument is optional. Even when the unused space is below the threshold (so a handler would normally be invoked), passing a NULL handler must not fault and the check must still succeed and report the unused space.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Set the threshold to the full stack size so the threshold is guaranteed to be crossed.\n- Run a full check with a NULL handler.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The check returns 0, reports a plausible unused value, and does not fault.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_runtime_stack_safety_full_check()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "runtime_stack_safety__test_stack_safety_full_check_null_handler"
        }
      ],
      "name": "runtime_stack_safety__test_stack_safety_full_check_null_handler",
      "summary": "Verify the full check tolerates a NULL handler when the threshold is crossed."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/runtime-stack-safety--test-stack-safety-threshold-bytes-set-get",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The byte form of the threshold setter must store the requested value verbatim, accept a threshold equal to the full stack size, and reject any value larger than the stack size.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Set the threshold to a small byte value and confirm it reads back exactly.\n- Set the threshold to the full stack size and confirm it is accepted.\n- Set the threshold to stack size + 1 and confirm it is rejected.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Valid byte thresholds return 0 and round-trip unchanged.\n- A threshold larger than the stack size returns -EINVAL.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_runtime_stack_unused_threshold_set()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_runtime_stack_unused_threshold_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "runtime_stack_safety__test_stack_safety_threshold_bytes_set_get"
        }
      ],
      "name": "runtime_stack_safety__test_stack_safety_threshold_bytes_set_get",
      "summary": "Verify the byte threshold setter round-trips and rejects oversized values."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/runtime-stack-safety--test-stack-safety-threshold-check",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The abbreviated threshold check performs a cheaper scan but must reach the same fire/no-fire decision as the full check for a given threshold, and when it fires it must report the same unused amount the full check measured.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Measure the unused space with a full check (threshold disabled).\n- Set the threshold just above that unused space, run an abbreviated check, and confirm the handler fires and the reported unused amount matches the full check.\n- Set the threshold below the unused space, run an abbreviated check, and confirm the handler does not fire.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The abbreviated check fires exactly when unused space is below the threshold and reports the same unused amount as the full check.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_runtime_stack_safety_threshold_check()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_runtime_stack_safety_full_check()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "runtime_stack_safety__test_stack_safety_threshold_check"
        }
      ],
      "name": "runtime_stack_safety__test_stack_safety_threshold_check",
      "summary": "Verify the abbreviated check fires like the full check and reports the same unused space."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/runtime-stack-safety--test-stack-safety-threshold-pct-set-get",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The percentage form of the threshold setter must convert the requested percentage of the thread's total stack size into an absolute byte count (the unit the kernel stores and reports), treat 0% as \"disabled\", and reject any percentage of 100% or more.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Set the threshold to 50% and 99% and confirm the byte value read back equals the matching fraction of the stack size.\n- Set the threshold to 0% and confirm it reads back as 0 (disabled).\n- Set the threshold to 100% and 200% and confirm both are rejected.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Valid percentages return 0 and round-trip to the equivalent byte count.\n- Percentages of 100% and above return -EINVAL.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_runtime_stack_unused_threshold_pct_set()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_runtime_stack_unused_threshold_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "runtime_stack_safety__test_stack_safety_threshold_pct_set_get"
        }
      ],
      "name": "runtime_stack_safety__test_stack_safety_threshold_pct_set_get",
      "summary": "Verify the percentage threshold setter stores bytes and rejects >= 100%."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/runtime-stack-safety--test-stack-safety-threshold-syscalls",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The threshold setters and getter are system calls. When userspace is enabled this test runs in user mode and so exercises the syscall verification path acting on the calling thread; when userspace is disabled it runs in supervisor mode and remains a valid functional check. The byte setter must round-trip, 0% must disable the threshold, and 100% must be rejected.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Set a byte threshold on the current thread and read it back.\n- Set the percentage threshold to 0% and confirm it reads back as 0.\n- Set the percentage threshold to 100% and confirm it is rejected.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The byte threshold round-trips, 0% disables the threshold, and 100% returns -EINVAL, all through the syscall interface.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_runtime_stack_unused_threshold_set()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_runtime_stack_unused_threshold_get()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_runtime_stack_unused_threshold_pct_set()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "runtime_stack_safety__test_stack_safety_threshold_syscalls"
        }
      ],
      "name": "runtime_stack_safety__test_stack_safety_threshold_syscalls",
      "summary": "Verify the threshold get/set syscalls operate correctly from user mode."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/semaphore--test-k-sem-correct-count-limit",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "- Reset an initialized semaphore's count to zero.\n- Give the semaphore by a thread and verify the semaphore's count is as expected.\n- Verify the max count a semaphore can reach.\n- Take the semaphore by a thread and verify the semaphore's count is as expected.\n- Verify the max times a semaphore can be taken.\n\n*See also:* k_sem_count_get()\n\n*See also:* k_sem_give()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "semaphore__test_k_sem_correct_count_limit"
        }
      ],
      "name": "semaphore__test_k_sem_correct_count_limit",
      "summary": "Test the max value a semaphore can be given and taken."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/semaphore--test-k-sem-define",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "- Get the semaphore count.\n- Verify the semaphore count equals to initialized value.\n\n*See also:* k_sem_count_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "semaphore__test_k_sem_define"
        }
      ],
      "name": "semaphore__test_k_sem_define",
      "summary": "Test semaphore defined at compile time."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/semaphore--test-k-sem-init-initial-count",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A semaphore initialized at run time with a non-zero initial count must actually hold that many \"units\": it can be taken exactly that many times without blocking, after which a further no-wait take fails with -EBUSY. This proves the initial value is set and semantically effective, rather than only being reported back by the count getter.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Initialize a semaphore with initial count 3 and a larger maximum.\n- Verify the reported count equals 3.\n- Take the semaphore three times with K_NO_WAIT; all must succeed.\n- Attempt a fourth no-wait take and verify it fails with -EBUSY.\n- Verify the count is now 0.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Exactly the initial number of takes succeed and the count tracks them.\n\n*See also:* k_sem_init(), k_sem_take(), k_sem_count_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "semaphore__test_k_sem_init_initial_count"
        }
      ],
      "name": "semaphore__test_k_sem_init_initial_count",
      "summary": "Verify k_sem_init() sets the initial semaphore count."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/semaphore--test-sem-count-get",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "k_sem_count_get() must return the current semaphore count without consuming it, and the reported count must track give/take operations: it increments on each give (saturating at the maximum) and decrements on each take.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Read the count right after initialization.\n- Give the semaphore and read the count again.\n- Repeatedly give up to (and beyond) the maximum and read the count.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The returned count matches the expected value at each step and never exceeds the configured maximum.\n\n*See also:* k_sem_count_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "semaphore__test_sem_count_get"
        }
      ],
      "name": "semaphore__test_sem_count_get",
      "summary": "Verify k_sem_count_get() reports the count without acquiring."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/semaphore--test-sem-give-from-isr",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "- Reset an initialized semaphore's count to zero\n- Create a loop, in each loop, do follow steps\n- Give the semaphore from an ISR\n- Get the semaphore's count\n- Verify whether the semaphore's count as expected\n\n*See also:* k_sem_give()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "semaphore__test_sem_give_from_isr"
        }
      ],
      "name": "semaphore__test_sem_give_from_isr",
      "summary": "Test whether a semaphore can be given by an ISR."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/semaphore--test-sem-give-from-thread",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "- Reset an initialized semaphore's count to zero\n- Create a loop, in each loop, do follow steps\n- Give the semaphore from a thread\n- Get the semaphore's count\n- Verify whether the semaphore's count as expected\n\n*See also:* k_sem_give()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "semaphore__test_sem_give_from_thread"
        }
      ],
      "name": "semaphore__test_sem_give_from_thread",
      "summary": "Test semaphore count when given by thread."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/semaphore--test-sem-give-take-from-isr",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Give the semaphore from an ISR up to its maximum, then take it from an ISR back down to zero, checking the count after every operation to confirm give/take work correctly from interrupt context.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Reset the semaphore so its count is zero.\n- Give from an ISR until the maximum count, checking the count each time.\n- Take from an ISR until zero, checking the count each time.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The count tracks every give/take and ends at zero.\n\n*See also:* k_sem_give()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "semaphore__test_sem_give_take_from_isr"
        }
      ],
      "name": "semaphore__test_sem_give_take_from_isr",
      "summary": "Verify a semaphore can be given and taken from ISR context."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/semaphore--test-sem-init-validity",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The existing test_k_sem_init() checks 3 hardcoded (init, max) combinations in a single test body. Here each combination is a separate named invocation so that Twister output immediately identifies which specific combination caused a failure (e.g. \"test_sem_init_validity[cases/3]\").\n\nValid combinations must return 0; invalid ones must return -EINVAL:\n\n- max == 0 -> -EINVAL\n- init > max -> -EINVAL\n- init == max (boundary) -> 0\n- init < max -> 0\n- large but valid max -> 0\n\n*See also:* k_sem_init()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "semaphore__test_sem_init_validity"
        }
      ],
      "name": "semaphore__test_sem_init_validity",
      "summary": "Parameterized k_sem_init() validity test."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/semaphore--test-sem-measure-timeout-from-thread",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A thread blocks on k_sem_take() with a one-second timeout while a lower priority thread gives the semaphore well before the timeout. The take must therefore return success in less than the full timeout period.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Reset the semaphores and start a helper thread that gives the semaphore.\n- Synchronize, then call k_sem_take() with a 1-second timeout.\n- Measure the elapsed time.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- k_sem_take() returns 0 and the elapsed time is well under one second.\n\n*See also:* k_sem_give()\n\n*See also:* k_sem_reset()\n\n*See also:* k_sem_take()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "semaphore__test_sem_measure_timeout_from_thread"
        }
      ],
      "name": "semaphore__test_sem_measure_timeout_from_thread",
      "summary": "Verify a timed k_sem_take() returns early when given by a thread."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/semaphore--test-sem-measure-timeouts",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "On an unavailable semaphore, k_sem_take() with a one-second timeout must block for at least that long before returning -EAGAIN, confirming the timeout period is actually observed (and a K_NO_WAIT take returns promptly).\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Reset the semaphore so its count is zero.\n- Record uptime, call k_sem_take() with a 1-second timeout, record uptime.\n- Verify the elapsed time is at least one second and the return is -EAGAIN.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The timed take blocks for >= 1 second and returns -EAGAIN.\n\n*See also:* k_sem_take()\n\n*See also:* k_sem_give()\n\n*See also:* k_sem_reset()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "semaphore__test_sem_measure_timeouts"
        }
      ],
      "name": "semaphore__test_sem_measure_timeouts",
      "summary": "Verify k_sem_take() honors the requested timeout duration."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/semaphore--test-sem-multi-take-timeout-diff-sem",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Threads block with different timeouts on two different semaphores. None are given, so every thread must time out (-EAGAIN), and the global expiry order must follow the ascending timeout values regardless of which semaphore each thread waited on.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Start threads waiting on two semaphores, each with a distinct timeout.\n- Record each expiry as it happens.\n- Verify the expiries occur in ascending timeout order.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- All takes return -EAGAIN and expire in ascending timeout order.\n\n*See also:* k_sem_take()\n\n*See also:* k_sem_reset()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "semaphore__test_sem_multi_take_timeout_diff_sem"
        }
      ],
      "name": "semaphore__test_sem_multi_take_timeout_diff_sem",
      "summary": "Verify timeout ordering across waiters on different semaphores."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/semaphore--test-sem-multiple-threads-wait",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Several threads block on the same semaphore. Giving the semaphore once per waiter must release each of them exactly once. The scenario is run twice to confirm the wait queue is left empty and reusable after the first round.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Start TOTAL_THREADS_WAITING threads, each blocking on the semaphore.\n- Give the semaphore once per waiting thread.\n- Confirm every thread acquired it and the counts return to zero.\n- Repeat the whole sequence a second time.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- All waiters are released each round and the wait queue ends empty.\n\n*See also:* k_sem_take()\n\n*See also:* k_sem_give()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "semaphore__test_sem_multiple_threads_wait"
        }
      ],
      "name": "semaphore__test_sem_multiple_threads_wait",
      "summary": "Verify all waiters are released as the semaphore is given repeatedly."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/semaphore--test-sem-reset",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "After giving a semaphore so its count is non-zero, k_sem_reset() must return the count to zero. A subsequent non-blocking take then fails with -EBUSY and a timed take fails with -EAGAIN, and the semaphore remains usable afterwards.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Initialize a semaphore and give it once (count becomes 1).\n- Call k_sem_reset() and read the count.\n- Attempt k_sem_take() with K_NO_WAIT and with a finite timeout.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Count is zero after reset; take returns -EBUSY (no wait) and -EAGAIN (timeout).\n\n*See also:* k_sem_reset()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "semaphore__test_sem_reset"
        }
      ],
      "name": "semaphore__test_sem_reset",
      "summary": "Verify k_sem_reset() sets the semaphore count back to zero."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/semaphore--test-sem-reset-waiting",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A waiter blocks on k_sem_take() with K_FOREVER while another thread resets the semaphore. The pending acquisition must be aborted and return -EAGAIN, and the semaphore must remain functional afterwards.\n\n*See also:* k_sem_reset()\n\n*See also:* k_sem_take()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "semaphore__test_sem_reset_waiting"
        }
      ],
      "name": "semaphore__test_sem_reset_waiting",
      "summary": "Verify that resetting a semaphore aborts pending acquisitions."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/semaphore--test-sem-take-multiple",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Several threads of differing priorities wait on the same semaphore. When the semaphore is given, the highest-priority (and, among equal priorities, the longest-waiting) thread must be the one that acquires it.\n\n*See also:* k_sem_take()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "semaphore__test_sem_take_multiple"
        }
      ],
      "name": "semaphore__test_sem_take_multiple",
      "summary": "Test semaphore take operation by multiple threads."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/semaphore--test-sem-take-no-wait",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "While the count is greater than zero, a non-blocking k_sem_take() must acquire the semaphore immediately and decrement the count by one on each call.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Give the semaphore several times to raise its count.\n- Repeatedly call k_sem_take() with K_NO_WAIT.\n- Read the count after each take.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Each take returns 0 and the count decreases by one each time.\n\n*See also:* k_sem_take()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "semaphore__test_sem_take_no_wait"
        }
      ],
      "name": "semaphore__test_sem_take_no_wait",
      "summary": "Verify k_sem_take() succeeds and decrements when the count is positive."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/semaphore--test-sem-take-no-wait-fails",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "When the count is zero and no timeout is provided (K_NO_WAIT), k_sem_take() must not block and must return -EBUSY, leaving the count unchanged at zero.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Reset the semaphore so its count is zero.\n- Call k_sem_take() with K_NO_WAIT repeatedly.\n- Read the count after each attempt.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Each take returns -EBUSY and the count remains zero.\n\n*See also:* k_sem_take()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "semaphore__test_sem_take_no_wait_fails"
        }
      ],
      "name": "semaphore__test_sem_take_no_wait_fails",
      "summary": "Verify k_sem_take() with no wait fails when the count is zero."
    },
