    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/stackprot--test-stackprot-canary-overflow",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "With CONFIG_STACK_CANARIES a function whose local buffer is overrun must be stopped when it returns, raising K_ERR_STACK_CHK_FAIL rather than letting corrupted stack contents be used. The overflowing thread would set a shared failure flag if it survived, and the suite's fatal error handler accepts only the stack-check reason, so both a missed overflow and a wrong error type are detected.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Spawn a thread that copies an over-long string into a 16-byte stack buffer.\n- Let it run; the canary check fails when the overflowed function returns.\n- The fatal error handler verifies the reason is K_ERR_STACK_CHK_FAIL.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The thread is terminated by the stack check and never reaches the code that would flag a failure.",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "stackprot__test_stackprot_canary_overflow"
        }
      ],
      "name": "stackprot__test_stackprot_canary_overflow",
      "summary": "Verify that a stack buffer overflow is caught by the canary."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/stackprot--test-stackprot-canary-scope",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "With CONFIG_STACK_CANARIES_TLS every thread gets its own canary value, so leaking one thread's canary does not help overflow another's; without it there is a single global canary shared by all threads. The spawned thread compares its own canary against its parent's, and which relation is correct depends on the configuration under test.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Read the current thread's canary value.\n- Spawn a thread, passing it that value, and have it compare against its own canary.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- With per-thread canaries the values differ; with a global canary they are identical.",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "stackprot__test_stackprot_canary_scope"
        }
      ],
      "name": "stackprot__test_stackprot_canary_scope",
      "summary": "Verify the scope of the stack canary value."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/stackprot--test-stackprot-no-false-positive",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The stack protector must be silent for well-behaved code: only an actual overflow may trigger it. This case runs the same buffer-handling function the overflow case uses, but with input that fits, and also checks that the overflow case's failure flag was never set the overflowing thread must have been stopped by the canary before it could reach the code that sets the flag. Ztest orders a suite alphabetically, so the overflow case (test_stackprot_canary_overflow) has already run when this check is made.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Check that the shared failure flag is still clear.\n- Call the buffer-copying function repeatedly with input that fits.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The calls complete normally and no stack check failure is raised.",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "stackprot__test_stackprot_no_false_positive"
        }
      ],
      "name": "stackprot__test_stackprot_no_false_positive",
      "summary": "Verify that legitimate stack use does not trip the canary."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/starve-fn--test-timer-starve",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Repeatedly start and stop timers in a tight, spinlock-protected loop for a sustained period and confirm that the system uptime and tick count keep advancing monotonically without regressing or overflowing.\n\n*See also:* k_uptime_get_32()\n\n*See also:* sys_clock_tick_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "starve_fn__test_timer_starve"
        }
      ],
      "name": "starve_fn__test_timer_starve",
      "summary": "Verify the uptime clock advances monotonically under timer starvation."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/suite-deadline--test-deadline",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "At equal priority the scheduler must order runnable threads by their deadline (set via k_thread_deadline_set()), running the earliest deadline first. Several equal-priority threads are given random deadlines and must execute in ascending-deadline order.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Create NUM_THREADS equal-priority threads, each recording its run order.\n- Assign each a random deadline, then sleep to let them run.\n- Verify their execution order matches ascending deadline order.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Threads execute earliest-deadline-first.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_deadline_set()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "suite_deadline__test_deadline"
        }
      ],
      "name": "suite_deadline__test_deadline",
      "summary": "Verify EDF threads run in earliest-deadline-first order."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/suite-deadline--test-thread-reschedule",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Changing a thread's deadline does not by itself trigger a context switch; an explicit k_reschedule() must re-evaluate the ready queue and switch to the now earliest-deadline thread. The test orchestrates two EDF helpers and checks the expected thread is selected only after k_reschedule() (exercised via a direct call and, on non-SMP, via irq_offload). Built for single-CPU configs only.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Create two EDF helper threads with different deadlines.\n- Confirm changing a deadline alone causes no switch.\n- Invoke k_reschedule() and confirm the expected thread is then selected.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The scheduler switches to the earliest-deadline thread only on reschedule.\n\n*See also:* k_reschedule()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_deadline_set()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "suite_deadline__test_thread_reschedule"
        }
      ],
      "name": "suite_deadline__test_thread_reschedule",
      "summary": "Verify a deadline change takes effect only on k_reschedule()."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/suite-deadline--test-unqueued",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Setting a deadline on a thread that is still in its start delay (unqueued) must not make it run before its delay elapses. Threads are created with a start delay, given deadlines while unqueued, and must stay dormant until the delay passes.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Create NUM_THREADS threads with a start delay (unqueued).\n- Set a random deadline on each and sleep less than the delay; none must run.\n- Sleep past the delay and confirm all threads then run.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Deadline-set does not start an unqueued thread early; threads run only after their start delay.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_deadline_set()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "suite_deadline__test_unqueued"
        }
      ],
      "name": "suite_deadline__test_unqueued",
      "summary": "Verify k_thread_deadline_set() does not wake a not-yet-started thread."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/suite-deadline--test-yield",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Threads at the same priority and deadline (0) must each get to run when they cooperatively yield. Each worker increments a counter and yields; all must have run by the time the test wakes.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Create NUM_THREADS equal-priority/deadline threads that bump a counter and k_yield().\n- Sleep and verify all threads ran.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Every thread runs, confirming yield rotates among equal peers.\n\n*See also:* k_yield()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "suite_deadline__test_yield"
        }
      ],
      "name": "suite_deadline__test_yield",
      "summary": "Verify k_yield() rotates among equal deadline/priority threads."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/suite-preempt--test-preempt",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Exhaustively checks which thread runs next when a thread is woken, across all priority classes (cooperative, preemptible, meta-IRQ). Threads in each class sleep; a low-priority manager wakes one, which wakes another, and the test validates the next-to-run thread matches the documented preemption rules. The check is repeated for all four combinations of holding/not holding the scheduler lock and for synchronous versus interrupt-context (offloaded) wakes. Single-CPU only.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Create paired threads at cooperative, preemptible and meta-IRQ priorities, all sleeping, plus a manager thread.\n- For each scenario, wake a thread that arranges to wake a target thread.\n- Validate the actually-scheduled thread against the expected one.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The next thread to run always matches the documented preemption rules for every class/lock/context combination.\n\n*See also:* k_wakeup()\n\n*See also:* k_sched_lock()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "suite_preempt__test_preempt"
        }
      ],
      "name": "suite_preempt__test_preempt",
      "summary": "Verify wakeups preempt the running thread per the documented rules."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/suite-preempt-metairq--test-preempt-metairq",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A meta-IRQ thread may preempt a running cooperative thread, but on completion the scheduler must resume that same cooperative thread rather than switching to another ready cooperative thread. The meta-IRQ unblocks a long-running low-priority coop thread, then unblocks a higher-priority coop thread before the first finishes; execution must continue in the low-priority thread and run the high-priority one only afterwards.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Start a meta-IRQ thread and two cooperative threads (low and high priority).\n- The meta-IRQ unblocks the low-priority coop thread, sleeps, then unblocks the high-priority coop thread mid-run.\n- Observe the order in which the coop threads complete.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The preempted low-priority coop thread resumes first; the high-priority coop thread runs after it, confirming the meta-IRQ returns to its preemptee.",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "suite_preempt_metairq__test_preempt_metairq"
        }
      ],
      "name": "suite_preempt_metairq__test_preempt_metairq",
      "summary": "Verify a meta-IRQ returns to the cooperative thread it preempted."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/sys-sem--test-sys-sem-basic",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "sys_sem_init() must validate its arguments and refuse to initialize a semaphore when they are nonsensical, returning -EINVAL rather than producing an unusable object. A valid initialization must then succeed and support a take/give cycle. This case is userspace-only.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Call sys_sem_init() with a NULL semaphore pointer.\n- Call sys_sem_init() with an initial count equal to the max (limit == 0).\n- Call sys_sem_init() with an initial count above the representable range.\n- Call sys_sem_init() with a limit above the representable range.\n- Finally initialize with valid values and perform a take then a give.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Every invalid call returns -EINVAL.\n- The valid initialization succeeds and the take/give cycle completes.\n\n*See also:* sys_sem_init()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "sys_sem__test_sys_sem_basic"
        }
      ],
      "name": "sys_sem__test_sys_sem_basic",
      "summary": "Verify sys_sem_init() rejects invalid arguments with -EINVAL."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/sys-sem--test-sys-sem-give-limit",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The count must saturate at the configured maximum: gives up to the limit succeed, and a give at the limit must report -EAGAIN without exceeding the maximum.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Initialize a semaphore with count 0 and max SEM_MAX_VAL.\n- Give SEM_MAX_VAL times, checking the count rises 1..MAX.\n- Give once more at the limit and inspect the return value and count.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Gives within the limit return 0; a give at the limit returns -EAGAIN and the count never exceeds SEM_MAX_VAL.\n\n*See also:* sys_sem_give()\n\n*See also:* sys_sem_count_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "sys_sem__test_sys_sem_give_limit"
        }
      ],
      "name": "sys_sem__test_sys_sem_give_limit",
      "summary": "Verify sys_sem_give() does not raise the count past its maximum."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/sys-sem--test-sys-sem-give-take-from-isr",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Repeated gives from interrupt context must raise the count to the maximum, and repeated takes from interrupt context must lower it back to zero, with the count accurate at every step.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Initialize a semaphore with count 0.\n- Give from an ISR SEM_MAX_VAL times, checking the count rises 1..MAX.\n- Take from an ISR SEM_MAX_VAL times, checking the count falls MAX-1..0.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The count matches the expected value after every give and take.\n\n*See also:* sys_sem_give()\n\n*See also:* sys_sem_take()\n\n*See also:* sys_sem_count_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "sys_sem__test_sys_sem_give_take_from_isr"
        }
      ],
      "name": "sys_sem__test_sys_sem_give_take_from_isr",
      "summary": "Verify give/take from an ISR track the count up and back down."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/sys-sem--test-sys-sem-simple-from-isr",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A sys_sem given from interrupt context must increase its available count by one per give, observable via sys_sem_count_get().\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Initialize a semaphore with count 0.\n- Give the semaphore from an ISR five times.\n- After each give, read the count.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The count equals the number of gives performed so far (1..5).\n\n*See also:* sys_sem_give()\n\n*See also:* sys_sem_count_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "sys_sem__test_sys_sem_simple_from_isr"
        }
      ],
      "name": "sys_sem__test_sys_sem_simple_from_isr",
      "summary": "Verify sys_sem_give() from an ISR increments the count."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/sys-sem--test-sys-sem-simple-from-task",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The same give-increments-count behavior must hold when invoked from a user-mode thread, exercising the userspace path of the API.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Initialize a semaphore with count 0.\n- Give the semaphore five times from the (user) test thread.\n- After each give, read the count.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The count equals the number of gives performed so far (1..5).\n\n*See also:* sys_sem_give()\n\n*See also:* sys_sem_count_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "sys_sem__test_sys_sem_simple_from_task"
        }
      ],
      "name": "sys_sem__test_sys_sem_simple_from_task",
      "summary": "Verify sys_sem_give() from a user thread increments the count."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/sys-sem--test-sys-sem-take-no-wait",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "When tokens are available, sys_sem_take() with K_NO_WAIT must succeed immediately and reduce the count by one each time.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Initialize a semaphore with count 5.\n- Take with K_NO_WAIT five times.\n- After each take, read the count.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Every take returns 0 and the count decrements 4, 3, 2, 1, 0.\n\n*See also:* sys_sem_take()\n\n*See also:* sys_sem_count_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "sys_sem__test_sys_sem_take_no_wait"
        }
      ],
      "name": "sys_sem__test_sys_sem_take_no_wait",
      "summary": "Verify sys_sem_take() succeeds and decrements when count is positive."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/sys-sem--test-sys-sem-take-no-wait-fails",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "With no tokens available, a non-blocking take must fail with -ETIMEDOUT and must not corrupt or change the count.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Initialize a semaphore with count 0.\n- Attempt a K_NO_WAIT take five times.\n- After each attempt, read the count.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Every take returns -ETIMEDOUT and the count stays 0.\n\n*See also:* sys_sem_take()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "sys_sem__test_sys_sem_take_no_wait_fails"
        }
      ],
      "name": "sys_sem__test_sys_sem_take_no_wait_fails",
      "summary": "Verify sys_sem_take() with K_NO_WAIT fails when count is zero."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/sys-sem--test-sys-sem-take-timeout",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A take blocked on a finite timeout must succeed if another thread gives the semaphore before the timeout expires.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Initialize a semaphore with count 0.\n- Start a thread that gives the semaphore.\n- In the test thread, take with a finite timeout (SEM_TIMEOUT).\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The take returns 0 (the give satisfied it before the timeout).\n\n*See also:* sys_sem_take()\n\n*See also:* sys_sem_give()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "sys_sem__test_sys_sem_take_timeout"
        }
      ],
      "name": "sys_sem__test_sys_sem_take_timeout",
      "summary": "Verify sys_sem_take() with a timeout succeeds when a give arrives."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/sys-sem-1cpu--test-sys-sem-multiple-threads-wait",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "When several threads block taking the same semaphore, one give must release exactly one waiter, so N gives release all N waiters and leave both semaphores drained. Repeated to confirm the behavior is stable across runs.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Initialize the semaphore with count 0.\n- Create TOTAL_THREADS_WAITING helper threads that each block taking the shared semaphore and then give a signalling semaphore.\n- Give the shared semaphore once per helper, then collect one signal per helper and check both semaphore counts.\n- Repeat the whole sequence twice.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Every helper is released (one per give); both counts end at 0 each round.\n\n*See also:* sys_sem_take()\n\n*See also:* sys_sem_give()\n\n*See also:* sys_sem_count_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "sys_sem_1cpu__test_sys_sem_multiple_threads_wait"
        }
      ],
      "name": "sys_sem_1cpu__test_sys_sem_multiple_threads_wait",
      "summary": "Verify multiple threads blocked on a sys_sem are each released by a give."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/sys-sem-1cpu--test-sys-sem-take-kernel-user",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A sys_sem given by a kernel thread must be takeable by a user thread and vice versa, demonstrating that the object works across the privilege boundary.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Initialize two semaphores with count 0.\n- Start a user thread that takes the first semaphore then gives the second.\n- The kernel test thread gives the first semaphore, then takes the second with a finite timeout.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The user thread's take succeeds and the kernel thread's take of the second semaphore returns 0.\n\n*See also:* sys_sem_take()\n\n*See also:* sys_sem_give()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "sys_sem_1cpu__test_sys_sem_take_kernel_user"
        }
      ],
      "name": "sys_sem_1cpu__test_sys_sem_take_kernel_user",
      "summary": "Verify a sys_sem is shared correctly between kernel and user threads."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/sys-sem-1cpu--test-sys-sem-take-multiple",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "When several threads of different priorities are all blocked taking the same semaphore, each give must release the highest-priority waiter first, so the threads complete strictly in priority order.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Create high-, mid-, and low-priority helper threads, each of which takes its own gate semaphore and then blocks taking the shared semaphore.\n- Lower the test thread's priority and release all three gates so the helpers pend on the shared semaphore.\n- Give the shared semaphore once and check which helper progressed; repeat.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The high-priority helper completes on the first give, the mid-priority on the second, and the low-priority on the third \u2014 never out of order.\n\n*See also:* sys_sem_take()\n\n*See also:* sys_sem_give()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "sys_sem_1cpu__test_sys_sem_take_multiple"
        }
      ],
      "name": "sys_sem_1cpu__test_sys_sem_take_multiple",
      "summary": "Verify pended sys_sem takers wake in thread-priority order."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/sys-sem-1cpu--test-sys-sem-take-timeout-fails",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "With no token available and no other thread giving, a take with a finite timeout must block for the timeout and then fail with -ETIMEDOUT.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Initialize a semaphore with count 0.\n- Take with a finite timeout (SEM_TIMEOUT) five times.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Every take returns -ETIMEDOUT after the timeout elapses.\n\n*See also:* sys_sem_take()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "sys_sem_1cpu__test_sys_sem_take_timeout_fails"
        }
      ],
      "name": "sys_sem_1cpu__test_sys_sem_take_timeout_fails",
      "summary": "Verify sys_sem_take() returns -ETIMEDOUT when its timeout expires."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/sys-sem-1cpu--test-sys-sem-take-timeout-forever",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A take with K_FOREVER must block indefinitely and wake only when another thread gives the semaphore, then succeed.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Initialize a semaphore with count 0.\n- Start a thread that sleeps briefly and then gives the semaphore.\n- In the test thread, take with K_FOREVER.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The take returns 0 once the helper thread gives the semaphore.\n\n*See also:* sys_sem_take()\n\n*See also:* sys_sem_give()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "sys_sem_1cpu__test_sys_sem_take_timeout_forever"
        }
      ],
      "name": "sys_sem_1cpu__test_sys_sem_take_timeout_forever",
      "summary": "Verify sys_sem_take() with K_FOREVER blocks until a give arrives."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/sys-sem-1cpu--test-sys-sem-take-timeout-isr",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A take blocked on a finite timeout must be woken and succeed when the semaphore is given from interrupt context.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Initialize a semaphore with count 0.\n- Start a thread that gives the semaphore from an ISR.\n- In the test thread, take with a finite timeout (SEM_TIMEOUT).\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The take returns 0 (satisfied by the ISR give before the timeout).\n\n*See also:* sys_sem_take()\n\n*See also:* sys_sem_give()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "sys_sem_1cpu__test_sys_sem_take_timeout_isr"
        }
      ],
      "name": "sys_sem_1cpu__test_sys_sem_take_timeout_isr",
      "summary": "Verify a timed sys_sem_take() is satisfied by a give from an ISR."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/syscalls--test-syscall-arg64",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Invoke system calls that take 64-bit arguments from user mode and verify the values received by the implementation match those passed in, confirming user threads can perform privileged operations through system calls with wide arguments.\n\n*See also:* syscall_arg64()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "syscalls__test_syscall_arg64"
        }
      ],
      "name": "syscalls__test_syscall_arg64",
      "summary": "Test passing 64-bit arguments through a system call."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/syscalls--test-syscall-context",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Some kernel code behaves differently depending on whether it was entered through a system call from user mode, so the predicate has to be true exactly then: not in an ordinary supervisor thread, not when the same function is invoked directly in supervisor mode, only inside a system call made by a user thread.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Check the predicate is false in the supervisor-mode test thread.\n- Invoke the test system call from supervisor mode and check it reports false inside.\n- Enter user mode and invoke the same call, checking it reports true.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The predicate is true only inside a system call invoked from user mode.\n\n*See also:* k_is_in_user_syscall()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "syscalls__test_syscall_context"
        }
      ],
      "name": "syscalls__test_syscall_context",
      "summary": "Verify that k_is_in_user_syscall() identifies user-invoked calls."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/syscalls--test-syscall-more-args",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Invoke a system call from user mode that takes more arguments than fit in registers and verify the implementation receives all of them intact, confirming user threads can perform privileged operations through system calls with many arguments.",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "syscalls__test_syscall_more_args"
        }
      ],
      "name": "syscalls__test_syscall_more_args",
      "summary": "Test passing the maximum number of system call arguments."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/syscalls--test-syscall-string-alloc-copy",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The allocating copy helper brings a user string into kernel memory before the kernel looks at it, so it must reject a string that does not match the expectation, one that exceeds the destination size, and one the caller has no right to read the kernel_string case, where the copy itself must fault. Only a readable string of acceptable length may be copied and compared successfully.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Pass a valid but unexpected string and check the comparison fails.\n- Pass an over-long string and check the copy is rejected.\n- Pass a kernel-owned string and check the copy faults.\n- Pass the expected string and check it copies and matches.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Each invalid input is rejected with its own error and only the readable, fitting string succeeds.\n\n*See also:* k_usermode_string_alloc_copy()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "syscalls__test_syscall_string_alloc_copy"
        }
      ],
      "name": "syscalls__test_syscall_string_alloc_copy",
      "summary": "Verify that k_usermode_string_alloc_copy() copies only readable, fitting strings."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/syscalls--test-syscall-string-copy",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The fixed-buffer counterpart of the allocating copy: same contract, but into a caller-provided buffer, with EINVAL for a string that does not fit and EFAULT for one the caller cannot read.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Pass a valid but unexpected string and check the comparison fails.\n- Pass an over-long string and check EINVAL is returned.\n- Pass a kernel-owned string and check EFAULT is returned.\n- Pass the expected string and check it copies and matches.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Each invalid input is rejected with its own error and only the readable, fitting string succeeds.\n\n*See also:* k_usermode_string_copy()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "syscalls__test_syscall_string_copy"
        }
      ],
      "name": "syscalls__test_syscall_string_copy",
      "summary": "Verify that k_usermode_string_copy() copies only readable, fitting strings."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/syscalls--test-syscall-string-nlen",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "String lengths handed to the kernel come from untrusted callers, so the measuring helper has to respect the caller's permissions: from user mode a kernel-owned string or a wild address must fault into the error argument rather than being read, while a string the caller owns measures normally. The same call from supervisor mode may read everything. The case runs in both modes via ZTEST_USER, so both sides of that contract are covered by one body.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Measure a kernel-owned string; expect a fault in user mode and a correct length in supervisor mode.\n- Measure a user-owned string; expect the correct length in both modes.\n- Measure a known-faulty address; expect a fault (skipped on platforms whose emulation cannot fault it).\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Lengths are returned only for memory the caller is allowed to read, and every forbidden access is reported through the error argument.\n\n*See also:* k_usermode_string_nlen()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "syscalls__test_syscall_string_nlen"
        }
      ],
      "name": "syscalls__test_syscall_string_nlen",
      "summary": "Verify that k_usermode_string_nlen() measures only memory the caller may read."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/syscalls--test-syscall-to-copy",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Copying data out of the kernel back to the caller must validate the destination the same way inbound copies validate the source: a buffer the user thread cannot write has to be refused with EFAULT, and a writable buffer receives the data intact.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Ask the kernel to copy into a kernel-owned buffer; expect EFAULT.\n- Ask it to copy into a stack buffer of the calling user thread.\n- Compare the received data with what the kernel was asked to send.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The unwritable destination is rejected and the writable one receives the exact data.\n\n*See also:* k_usermode_to_copy()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "syscalls__test_syscall_to_copy"
        }
      ],
      "name": "syscalls__test_syscall_to_copy",
      "summary": "Verify that k_usermode_to_copy() writes only to caller-writable memory."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/syscalls-extended--test-syscall-switch-stress",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The entry and exit paths of a system call touch per-CPU and per-thread state that has to hold up when every CPU is crossing the privilege boundary at once. Four user threads per CPU hammer the string-handling test calls in a tight loop, yielding periodically so the schedulers on all CPUs keep moving threads around; corruption anywhere in the path shows up as a failed validation inside one of the calls.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Spawn four user threads per CPU running the string system calls in a loop.\n- Let them run for the configured iteration count and join them all.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Every thread completes every iteration without a validation failure or fault.",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "syscalls_extended__test_syscall_switch_stress"
        }
      ],
      "name": "syscalls_extended__test_syscall_switch_stress",
      "summary": "Stress the system call path with many concurrent user threads."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/test-log-core-additional--test-log-backend",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A logging backend can be brought online and taken offline at runtime so that log messages are routed to (or withheld from) a given system resource. The test exercises the backend lifecycle API and confirms the active state is reported correctly after each transition.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Initialize the logging core.\n- Activate the test backend and confirm it reports as active.\n- Deactivate it and confirm it reports as inactive.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- log_backend_is_active() tracks the activate/deactivate transitions.\n\n*See also:* log_backend_activate()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "test_log_core_additional__test_log_backend"
        }
      ],
      "name": "test_log_core_additional__test_log_backend",
      "summary": "Verify dynamic activation and deactivation of a logging backend."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/test-log-core-additional--test-log-early-logging",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "In deferred mode log messages are queued by the core and processed later, so messages generated before any backend is enabled must not be lost. The test emits messages while no backend is active, then enables a backend and confirms all the buffered messages are eventually delivered.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Skip in immediate mode; otherwise initialize the core and deactivate all backends.\n- Emit info, warning and error messages with no backend active.\n- Enable the test backend, process the queue and confirm all messages arrive.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Every message logged before backend activation is delivered after it.\n\n*See also:* LOG_INF()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "test_log_core_additional__test_log_early_logging"
        }
      ],
      "name": "test_log_core_additional__test_log_early_logging",
      "summary": "Verify messages logged before a backend is active are buffered and later delivered."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/test-log-core-additional--test-log-filter-set",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "log_filter_set() adjusts the runtime severity threshold for a source/backend combination at runtime. The test invokes it from a user-mode thread to set the warning level for the local domain, confirming runtime filtering is reachable and accepted from user space.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- From user space, call log_filter_set() with LOG_LEVEL_WRN for the local domain.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The runtime filter is applied without fault.\n\n*See also:* log_filter_set()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "test_log_core_additional__test_log_filter_set"
        }
      ],
      "name": "test_log_core_additional__test_log_filter_set",
      "summary": "Verify runtime severity filtering can be configured from user space."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/test-log-core-additional--test-log-from-user",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A user-mode thread emits log messages that are buffered by the core and processed later. The test records the buffered message count, drains the queue and confirms the buffered count does not increase, demonstrating that user-space messages are queued and subsequently processed.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- From user space, emit a plain and a formatted info message.\n- Capture the buffered count, run log_process() to drain the queue.\n- Confirm the post-processing buffered count is not greater than before.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- User-space messages are buffered and then processed off the queue.\n\n*See also:* log_process()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "test_log_core_additional__test_log_from_user"
        }
      ],
      "name": "test_log_core_additional__test_log_from_user",
      "summary": "Verify messages can be logged and deferred-processed from user space."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/test-log-core-additional--test-log-generic",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "log_generic() accepts a format string and a va_list, letting callers emit log messages with printf-style argument substitution. The test issues several messages with differing format specifiers and argument counts and confirms the core accepts and processes each one.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Set up the logging core with a single backend.\n- Call log_generic() with no-arg, string and integer format variants.\n- Process the log queue.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- All formatted messages are accepted and processed without error.\n\n*See also:* log_generic()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "test_log_core_additional__test_log_generic"
        }
      ],
      "name": "test_log_core_additional__test_log_generic",
      "summary": "Verify log_generic() renders messages with printf-style arguments."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/test-log-core-additional--test-log-generic-user",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "log_generic() accepts a format string and a va_list and performs printf-style rendering of the message. The test calls it from a user-mode thread and processes the queue, confirming formatted logging works across the user/kernel boundary.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- From user space, call log_generic() with a format string.\n- Process the log queue.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The formatted message is accepted and processed without fault.\n\n*See also:* log_generic()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "test_log_core_additional__test_log_generic_user"
        }
      ],
      "name": "test_log_core_additional__test_log_generic_user",
      "summary": "Verify log_generic() renders formatted messages from user space."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/test-log-core-additional--test-log-hexdump-from-user",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The logging system can render a buffer of binary data as a hexadecimal dump. The test issues a LOG_HEXDUMP_INF() of a small data buffer from a user-mode thread and processes the queue, confirming the hexdump path is exercised across the user/kernel boundary.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- From user space, hexdump a 4-byte value via LOG_HEXDUMP_INF().\n- Process the log queue.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The binary data is logged as a hex dump without fault.\n\n*See also:* LOG_HEXDUMP_INF()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "test_log_core_additional__test_log_hexdump_from_user"
        }
      ],
      "name": "test_log_core_additional__test_log_hexdump_from_user",
      "summary": "Verify binary data can be logged as a hex dump from user space."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/test-log-core-additional--test-log-panic",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "log_panic() forces the logging subsystem into synchronous mode and flushes all pending messages, as used before halting on a fatal error. The test emits messages from a user-mode thread and then calls log_panic() to confirm the flush path is reachable and completes from user space.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- From user space, emit two info messages.\n- Call log_panic() to flush pending messages.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Pending messages are flushed and the panic path completes without fault.\n\n*See also:* log_panic()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "test_log_core_additional__test_log_panic"
        }
      ],
      "name": "test_log_core_additional__test_log_panic",
      "summary": "Verify log_panic() flushes pending messages from user space."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/test-log-core-additional--test-log-printk-from-user",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "LOG_PRINTK() funnels printk-style output into the logging subsystem so it can be processed alongside regular log messages. The test issues a LOG_PRINTK() from a user-mode thread and drains the queue, confirming the path is reachable from user space.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- From user space, emit output via LOG_PRINTK().\n- Process the log queue.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The printk-style output is processed without fault.\n\n*See also:* LOG_PRINTK()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "test_log_core_additional__test_log_printk_from_user"
        }
      ],
      "name": "test_log_core_additional__test_log_printk_from_user",
      "summary": "Verify LOG_PRINTK() routes printk-style output through logging from user space."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/test-log-core-additional--test-log-severity",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The module is registered at LOG_LEVEL_INF, so debug messages are stripped from the build while info, warning and error messages are retained. The test emits one message at each retained level and confirms the backend receives them with the correct severity, demonstrating both multi-level severity support and compile-time level filtering.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Set up the logging core with a backend that checks severity.\n- Emit info, warning and error messages.\n- Process the log and confirm each message carries its expected severity.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Three messages are delivered, each tagged with its severity level.\n\n*See also:* LOG_INF()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "test_log_core_additional__test_log_severity"
        }
      ],
      "name": "test_log_core_additional__test_log_severity",
      "summary": "Verify messages are delivered with their severity level and below-level messages are removed at compile time."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/test-log-core-additional--test-log-sync",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "In immediate mode each log message is rendered and delivered in the context that generated it rather than being queued for deferred processing. The test emits messages with CONFIG_LOG_MODE_IMMEDIATE enabled and confirms the backend handled them synchronously without an explicit processing step.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Skip the test unless CONFIG_LOG_MODE_IMMEDIATE is enabled.\n- Set up the logging core and emit two informational messages.\n- Confirm both were handled synchronously by the backend.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Both messages are delivered immediately, with no deferred processing.\n\n*See also:* LOG_INF()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "test_log_core_additional__test_log_sync"
        }
      ],
      "name": "test_log_core_additional__test_log_sync",
      "summary": "Verify messages are processed immediately in immediate logging mode."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/test-log-core-additional--test-log-thread",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "When the dedicated logging thread is enabled, messages are buffered and processed asynchronously by that thread, isolating log processing from the contexts that generate messages. The test emits messages, confirms data is pending, then waits for the logging thread to drain the queue.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Set up the logging core and confirm no data is pending.\n- Emit info, warning and error messages and confirm data is now pending.\n- Wait for the logging thread to process and confirm the queue is drained.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The dedicated logging thread delivers all messages and clears the queue.\n\n*See also:* log_data_pending()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "test_log_core_additional__test_log_thread"
        }
      ],
      "name": "test_log_core_additional__test_log_thread",
      "summary": "Verify queued log messages are processed by the dedicated logging thread."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/test-log-core-additional--test-log-thread-trigger",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The dedicated logging thread normally processes buffered messages on its own schedule, but it can be triggered to drain the queue as soon as possible. The test emits messages, confirms they are pending, triggers the thread and confirms the queue is drained shortly afterwards.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Set up the logging core and confirm no data is pending.\n- Emit info, warning and error messages and confirm data is pending.\n- Call log_thread_trigger(), then confirm the queue is drained.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Triggering the logging thread promptly delivers all queued messages.\n\n*See also:* log_thread_trigger()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "test_log_core_additional__test_log_thread_trigger"
        }
      ],
      "name": "test_log_core_additional__test_log_thread_trigger",
      "summary": "Verify the logging thread can be triggered to process queued messages immediately."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/test-log-core-additional--test-log-timestamping",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The logging core allows the application to register a custom timestamp source and frequency so that each message records the time it was generated. The test registers a counter-based timestamp function, emits messages and confirms each one is stamped with the expected monotonically increasing value, and that a NULL function is rejected.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Register a custom timestamp function (and verify NULL is rejected).\n- Enable the backend with timestamp checking and expected values.\n- Emit three messages and confirm each carries its expected timestamp.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Each delivered message carries the timestamp produced by the custom source.\n\n*See also:* log_set_timestamp_func()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "test_log_core_additional__test_log_timestamping"
        }
      ],
      "name": "test_log_core_additional__test_log_timestamping",
      "summary": "Verify a user-supplied timestamp function stamps log messages."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/test-log-core-additional--test-multiple-backends",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Log messages can be routed to several backends (system resources) at once. The test enables two test backends and confirms at least two backends are registered, and additionally that the UART backend is present when configured, demonstrating that logging can target multiple destinations simultaneously.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Enable both test backends via the setup helper.\n- Count the registered backends and assert there are at least two.\n- When CONFIG_LOG_BACKEND_UART is set, confirm the UART backend is present.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Multiple backends are registered and discoverable concurrently.\n\n*See also:* log_backend_enable()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "test_log_core_additional__test_multiple_backends"
        }
      ],
      "name": "test_log_core_additional__test_multiple_backends",
      "summary": "Verify the logging system supports multiple concurrent backends."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/test-log-output--test-colors",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Enables the level and colors flags and confirms the formatter surrounds each message with the ANSI color escape sequence associated with its severity (or the default sequence when colors are disabled in the build). This validates color decoration of formatted, severity-tagged output.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- For each level, build the expected colored string and package a message.\n- Process it with the level + colors flags set.\n- Assert the rendered length and string for each level.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Each message is wrapped with the expected color codes for its level.\n\n*See also:* log_output_process()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "test_log_output__test_colors"
        }
      ],
      "name": "test_log_output__test_colors",
      "summary": "Verify color escape codes wrap messages per severity when enabled."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/test-log-output--test-format-ts",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Sets the timestamp frequency and enables both the timestamp and format-timestamp flags so the raw tick value is converted into a human and machine readable date/time string (plain, date, or ISO8601 per Kconfig). This validates timestamp formatting that supports post-processing of logs.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Set the output timestamp frequency to 1 MHz.\n- Package a simple string and process it with timestamp + format flags.\n- Compare the rendered buffer against the expected formatted timestamp.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Output is prefixed with the configured formatted timestamp string.\n\n*See also:* log_output_process()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "test_log_output__test_format_ts"
        }
      ],
      "name": "test_log_output__test_format_ts",
      "summary": "Verify a timestamp is rendered as a formatted date/time string."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/test-log-output--test-level-flag",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Enables LOG_OUTPUT_FLAG_LEVEL and confirms the formatter prepends the textual severity marker (e.g. \"<inf>\") ahead of the domain/source prefix. This validates rendering of the message severity into the formatted output.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Package a simple string with cbprintf_package().\n- Process it at LOG_LEVEL_INF with the level flag set.\n- Compare the rendered buffer against the expected string.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Output equals \"<inf> domain/src: test\\r\\n\".\n\n*See also:* log_output_process()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "test_log_output__test_level_flag"
        }
      ],
      "name": "test_log_output__test_level_flag",
      "summary": "Verify the severity level marker is rendered when requested."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/test-log-output--test-levels",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Iterates over the error, warning, info and debug levels and confirms the formatter renders the matching marker (\"<err>\", \"<wrn>\", \"<inf>\", \"<dbg>\"), optionally including the function-name prefix when configured. This validates rendering of all supported severity levels into formatted output.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- For each level, package a message and process it with the level flag.\n- Account for the function-name prefix when enabled for that level.\n- Assert the rendered length and string for each level.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Each message carries the correct severity marker and text.\n\n*See also:* log_output_process()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "test_log_output__test_levels"
        }
      ],
      "name": "test_log_output__test_levels",
      "summary": "Verify each severity level renders its corresponding text marker."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/test-log-output--test-no-flags",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Drives the log_output formatter with zero output flags to confirm that the baseline rendering of a message is \"<source>: <text>\" terminated by CRLF. This exercises the printf-style formatting of a log message into text with the default decorations only.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Package a simple string with cbprintf_package().\n- Process it through log_output with flags = 0.\n- Compare the rendered buffer against the expected string.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Output equals \"src: test\\r\\n\".\n\n*See also:* log_output_process()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "test_log_output__test_no_flags"
        }
      ],
      "name": "test_log_output__test_no_flags",
      "summary": "Verify a log message renders with source name and no extra prefixes."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/test-log-output--test-no-flags-dname",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Supplies both a domain name and a source name to the formatter and confirms the rendered message is prefixed with \"domain/src:\". This validates that the formatter composes the message prefix from domain and source identifiers.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Package a simple string with cbprintf_package().\n- Process it through log_output supplying both DNAME and SNAME.\n- Compare the rendered buffer against the expected string.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Output equals \"domain/src: test\\r\\n\".\n\n*See also:* log_output_process()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "test_log_output__test_no_flags_dname"
        }
      ],
      "name": "test_log_output__test_no_flags_dname",
      "summary": "Verify a log message renders with domain and source name prefix."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/test-log-output--test-raw",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Processes a message tagged as an internal raw string and confirms the formatter emits only the payload text, with no source prefix, level marker, or trailing CRLF. This validates the formatter's handling of unformatted passthrough content.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Package a simple string with cbprintf_package().\n- Process it through log_output with LOG_LEVEL_INTERNAL_RAW_STRING.\n- Compare the rendered buffer against the raw payload.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Output equals \"test\" with no added prefix or newline.\n\n*See also:* log_output_process()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "test_log_output__test_raw"
        }
      ],
      "name": "test_log_output__test_raw",
      "summary": "Verify a raw-string log message is rendered without any decoration."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/test-log-output--test-skip-src",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Enables LOG_OUTPUT_FLAG_SKIP_SOURCE and confirms the formatter renders only the message payload and CRLF, without the source-name prefix. This validates the formatter's ability to suppress source decoration in the output.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Package a simple string with cbprintf_package().\n- Process it with the skip-source flag set.\n- Compare the rendered buffer against the expected string.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Output equals \"test\\r\\n\" with no source prefix.\n\n*See also:* log_output_process()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "test_log_output__test_skip_src"
        }
      ],
      "name": "test_log_output__test_skip_src",
      "summary": "Verify the source prefix is omitted when the skip-source flag is set."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/test-log-output--test-thread-id",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Enables the thread flag and confirms the formatter prepends the originating thread's priority and name (or pointer when names are disabled) to the message. This validates rendering of thread context into formatted output.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Skip unless CONFIG_LOG_THREAD_ID_PREFIX is enabled.\n- Build the expected string from the current thread's priority and name.\n- Package a message and process it with the level + thread flags.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Output includes the \"[prio name]\" thread prefix ahead of the message.\n\n*See also:* log_output_process()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "test_log_output__test_thread_id"
        }
      ],
      "name": "test_log_output__test_thread_id",
      "summary": "Verify the originating thread identity is rendered when requested."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/test-log-output--test-ts-flag",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Enables LOG_OUTPUT_FLAG_TIMESTAMP and confirms the formatter prepends the numeric timestamp in brackets, sized according to the 32/64-bit timestamp configuration. A timestamped, machine-readable prefix supports later post-processing of the log stream.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Package a simple string with cbprintf_package().\n- Process it with the timestamp flag set.\n- Compare the rendered buffer against the expected bracketed timestamp.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Output is prefixed with \"[00000000] \" (or 64-bit form) followed by the message.\n\n*See also:* log_output_process()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "test_log_output__test_ts_flag"
        }
      ],
      "name": "test_log_output__test_ts_flag",
      "summary": "Verify a raw timestamp is rendered when the timestamp flag is set."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/test-log-output--test-ts-to-us",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Exercises the timestamp-to-microseconds conversion helper at two different timestamp frequencies and confirms the scaled results. Correct conversion underpins the timestamps emitted into formatted log output.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Set frequency to 1 MHz and convert 1000 ticks.\n- Set frequency to 32768 Hz and convert 10 ticks.\n- Assert the converted microsecond values.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Conversions yield 1000 us and 305 us respectively.\n\n*See also:* log_output_timestamp_to_us()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "test_log_output__test_ts_to_us"
        }
      ],
      "name": "test_log_output__test_ts_to_us",
      "summary": "Verify timestamp ticks are converted to microseconds correctly."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/test-log-output-net--test-format",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Drives the log_output formatter with the syslog format flag (as used by the network backend) and confirms the rendered message follows the syslog wire format: priority, version, ISO timestamp, host, and the domain/source message body. This validates the structured, machine-parseable output produced for the network (syslog) logging backend.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Set the output timestamp frequency to 1 MHz.\n- Package a message and process it with timestamp, format-timestamp and syslog format flags.\n- Compare the rendered buffer against the expected syslog frame.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Output equals the expected \"<134>1 ... domain/src: test\" syslog string.\n\n*See also:* log_output_process()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "test_log_output_net__test_format"
        }
      ],
      "name": "test_log_output_net__test_format",
      "summary": "Verify a log message is rendered in RFC 5424 syslog format."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/test-timestamp--test-custom-timestamp",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Registers a custom timestamp callback and confirms that, when custom timestamp formatting is enabled, the formatter emits the callback's text in place of the default numeric timestamp; otherwise it falls back to the default bracketed timestamp. The rendered timestamp supports later post-processing of the log stream.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Register custom_timestamp() when custom-timestamp formatting is enabled.\n- Package a message and process it with the timestamp flag set.\n- Compare the rendered buffer against the expected (custom or default) form.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Output uses the custom timestamp text when enabled, else the default numeric timestamp.\n\n*See also:* log_custom_timestamp_set()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "test_timestamp__test_custom_timestamp"
        }
      ],
      "name": "test_timestamp__test_custom_timestamp",
      "summary": "Verify a user-supplied custom timestamp formatter feeds log output."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/thread-dynamic--test-dyn-thread-create-from-kernel",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A thread object obtained from k_object_alloc() must be usable as the target of k_thread_create() exactly like a statically defined one, and the thread created from it must be able to run and use the objects it was granted. The created thread runs to completion through a pair of semaphores, so a dynamic object that is mis-initialized shows up as the handshake failing rather than as a silent no-op. Skipped without userspace, where dynamic kernel objects do not exist.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Allocate a thread object with k_object_alloc() from the kernel-mode test thread.\n- Create a user thread from it and grant it both semaphores.\n- Start the thread, give the start semaphore and wait on the end semaphore.\n- Abort the thread and release the object.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The allocation succeeds and the dynamic user thread completes the handshake within the timeout.\n\n*See also:* k_object_alloc()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_create()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "thread_dynamic__test_dyn_thread_create_from_kernel"
        }
      ],
      "name": "thread_dynamic__test_dyn_thread_create_from_kernel",
      "summary": "Verify that a kernel thread can create a dynamic user thread."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/thread-dynamic--test-dyn-thread-create-from-user",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The same dynamic creation path has to work when the caller is itself a user thread, where k_object_alloc() and the grants that follow it are reached through system calls and are subject to the caller's own permissions. The check is therefore repeated with the test case running in user mode.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Allocate a thread object with k_object_alloc() from a user-mode test thread.\n- Create a user thread from it and grant it both semaphores.\n- Start the thread, give the start semaphore and wait on the end semaphore.\n- Abort the thread and release the object.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The allocation succeeds and the dynamic user thread completes the handshake within the timeout.\n\n*See also:* k_object_alloc()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_create()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "thread_dynamic__test_dyn_thread_create_from_user"
        }
      ],
      "name": "thread_dynamic__test_dyn_thread_create_from_user",
      "summary": "Verify that a user thread can create a dynamic user thread."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/thread-error-case--test-thread-create-essential-denied",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Aborting an essential thread panics the kernel, so unprivileged code must not be able to set K_ESSENTIAL on a thread it creates.\n\nAll cases in this suite share one mechanism: a worker thread, running in user mode when the test case does, arms the ztest fatal-error hook and then makes the offending call. The call must not return reaching the code after it fails the test so a pass means the error really was caught.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Spawn a worker thread and arm the ztest fatal-error hook.\n- Make the offending call from that thread.\n- Join the worker thread.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The call raises the expected fatal error and never returns.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_create()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "thread_error_case__test_thread_create_essential_denied"
        }
      ],
      "name": "thread_error_case__test_thread_create_essential_denied",
      "summary": "Verify that a user thread cannot create an essential thread."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/thread-error-case--test-thread-create-null-stack",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The verifier requires the stack to be a valid, uninitialized kernel stack object, so a NULL pointer must be refused rather than used as a stack.\n\nAll cases in this suite share one mechanism: a worker thread, running in user mode when the test case does, arms the ztest fatal-error hook and then makes the offending call. The call must not return reaching the code after it fails the test so a pass means the error really was caught.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Spawn a worker thread and arm the ztest fatal-error hook.\n- Make the offending call from that thread.\n- Join the worker thread.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The call raises the expected fatal error and never returns.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_create()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "thread_error_case__test_thread_create_null_stack"
        }
      ],
      "name": "thread_error_case__test_thread_create_null_stack",
      "summary": "Verify that k_thread_create() rejects a NULL stack object."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/thread-error-case--test-thread-create-null-thread",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The verifier requires the new thread to be a valid, uninitialized kernel object, so a NULL pointer must be refused rather than initialized.\n\nAll cases in this suite share one mechanism: a worker thread, running in user mode when the test case does, arms the ztest fatal-error hook and then makes the offending call. The call must not return reaching the code after it fails the test so a pass means the error really was caught.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Spawn a worker thread and arm the ztest fatal-error hook.\n- Make the offending call from that thread.\n- Join the worker thread.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The call raises the expected fatal error and never returns.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_create()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "thread_error_case__test_thread_create_null_thread"
        }
      ],
      "name": "thread_error_case__test_thread_create_null_thread",
      "summary": "Verify that k_thread_create() rejects a NULL new-thread object."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/thread-error-case--test-thread-create-stack-size-overflow",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The verifier adds K_THREAD_STACK_RESERVED to the requested size, so a size of SIZE_MAX wraps around; the overflow has to be detected instead of yielding a small, bogus stack.\n\nAll cases in this suite share one mechanism: a worker thread, running in user mode when the test case does, arms the ztest fatal-error hook and then makes the offending call. The call must not return reaching the code after it fails the test so a pass means the error really was caught.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Spawn a worker thread and arm the ztest fatal-error hook.\n- Make the offending call from that thread.\n- Join the worker thread.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The call raises the expected fatal error and never returns.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_create()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "thread_error_case__test_thread_create_stack_size_overflow"
        }
      ],
      "name": "thread_error_case__test_thread_create_stack_size_overflow",
      "summary": "Verify that k_thread_create() rejects a stack size that overflows."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/thread-error-case--test-thread-create-supervisor-denied",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Omitting K_USER from a thread created in user context would hand unprivileged code a privileged thread, so the verifier must require the flag on every thread spawned from user mode.\n\nAll cases in this suite share one mechanism: a worker thread, running in user mode when the test case does, arms the ztest fatal-error hook and then makes the offending call. The call must not return reaching the code after it fails the test so a pass means the error really was caught.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Spawn a worker thread and arm the ztest fatal-error hook.\n- Make the offending call from that thread.\n- Join the worker thread.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The call raises the expected fatal error and never returns.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_create()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "thread_error_case__test_thread_create_supervisor_denied"
        }
      ],
      "name": "thread_error_case__test_thread_create_supervisor_denied",
      "summary": "Verify that a user thread cannot create a supervisor thread."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/thread-error-case--test-thread-float-disable-null",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The syscall verification layer must reject a NULL thread object before it reaches the kernel, where it would be dereferenced.\n\nAll cases in this suite share one mechanism: a worker thread, running in user mode when the test case does, arms the ztest fatal-error hook and then makes the offending call. The call must not return reaching the code after it fails the test so a pass means the error really was caught.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Spawn a worker thread and arm the ztest fatal-error hook.\n- Make the offending call from that thread.\n- Join the worker thread.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The call raises the expected fatal error and never returns.\n\n*See also:* k_float_disable()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "thread_error_case__test_thread_float_disable_null"
        }
