          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "thread_error_case__test_thread_timeout_remaining_ticks_null"
        }
      ],
      "name": "thread_error_case__test_thread_timeout_remaining_ticks_null",
      "summary": "Verify that k_thread_timeout_remaining_ticks() rejects a NULL thread pointer."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/thread-error-case--test-thread-wakeup-null",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The syscall verification layer must reject a NULL thread object before it reaches the kernel, where it would be dereferenced.\n\nAll cases in this suite share one mechanism: a worker thread, running in user mode when the test case does, arms the ztest fatal-error hook and then makes the offending call. The call must not return reaching the code after it fails the test so a pass means the error really was caught.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Spawn a worker thread and arm the ztest fatal-error hook.\n- Make the offending call from that thread.\n- Join the worker thread.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The call raises the expected fatal error and never returns.\n\n*See also:* k_wakeup()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "thread_error_case__test_thread_wakeup_null"
        }
      ],
      "name": "thread_error_case__test_thread_wakeup_null",
      "summary": "Verify that k_wakeup() rejects a NULL thread pointer."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/thread-init--test-thread-init-create-coop",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The cooperative counterpart of the run-time creation case, covering a negative priority and the K_USER option. It is also the case that exercises the start delay: the thread is created with a two second delay and knows when it was created, so it can confirm it was not made ready early. Skipped when userspace is not enabled, as the thread is created with the user option that configuration does not provide.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Record the creation timestamp, then create a cooperative thread with a two second start delay.\n- Release it from its start semaphore.\n- In the thread, check that the time from creation to its first execution is at least the requested delay, then compare its priority and its p1, p2 and p3 against the requested values and signal completion.\n- Wait for the completion semaphore.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Creation succeeds, the thread starts no earlier than the requested delay, and it runs at the requested cooperative priority with the requested parameters.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_create()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_priority_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "thread_init__test_thread_init_create_coop"
        }
      ],
      "name": "thread_init__test_thread_init_create_coop",
      "summary": "Verify that k_thread_create() honours priority, parameters and start delay for a cooperative thread."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/thread-init--test-thread-init-create-preempt",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Creating a thread at run time must apply everything the call specifies: the priority, the three parameters and the delay before the thread is made ready. This case creates the thread with a delay of zero, so it covers immediate start; the cooperative case below carries the non-zero delay.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Record the creation timestamp, then create a preemptible thread with a zero start delay.\n- Release it from its start semaphore.\n- In the thread, check that the time from creation to its first execution is at least the requested delay, then compare its priority and its p1, p2 and p3 against the requested values and signal completion.\n- Wait for the completion semaphore.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Creation succeeds and the thread runs at the requested priority with the requested parameters.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_create()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_priority_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "thread_init__test_thread_init_create_preempt"
        }
      ],
      "name": "thread_init__test_thread_init_create_preempt",
      "summary": "Verify that k_thread_create() honours priority, parameters and start delay for a preemptible thread."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/thread-init--test-thread-init-kdefine-coop",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The cooperative counterpart of the preemptible case: a negative priority must survive static definition just as a positive one does, so the same checks are repeated against a thread defined at a cooperative priority.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Record the priority and parameters the thread was defined with.\n- Release the statically defined cooperative thread from its start semaphore.\n- In the thread, compare its own priority and its p1, p2 and p3 against the recorded values, then signal completion.\n- Wait for the completion semaphore.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The thread runs at the defined cooperative priority and receives the defined parameters.\n\n*See also:* K_THREAD_DEFINE()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_priority_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "thread_init__test_thread_init_kdefine_coop"
        }
      ],
      "name": "thread_init__test_thread_init_kdefine_coop",
      "summary": "Verify that K_THREAD_DEFINE() initializes a cooperative thread with the values it was given."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/thread-init--test-thread-init-kdefine-preempt",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A statically defined thread has to reach its entry point with exactly the priority and the three parameters named in its definition, the same way a thread created at run time does. The thread itself performs the checks, so what is validated is the state the kernel actually handed it rather than what the definition asked for. The start delay is not checked here because a statically defined thread has no observable creation timestamp.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Record the priority and parameters the thread was defined with.\n- Release the statically defined preemptible thread from its start semaphore.\n- In the thread, compare its own priority and its p1, p2 and p3 against the recorded values, then signal completion.\n- Wait for the completion semaphore.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The thread runs at the defined priority and receives the defined parameters.\n\n*See also:* K_THREAD_DEFINE()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_priority_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "thread_init__test_thread_init_kdefine_preempt"
        }
      ],
      "name": "thread_init__test_thread_init_kdefine_preempt",
      "summary": "Verify that K_THREAD_DEFINE() initializes a preemptible thread with the values it was given."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/thread-tls--test-tls-vars-are-per-thread",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A variable declared thread-local must resolve to a separate instance per thread, so writes made by one thread are invisible to every other one. Several threads run the same body concurrently, each writing a value derived from its own identity and then checking that what it reads back is still its own; a shared instance would show up as a thread observing a neighbour's value. Skipped when userspace is enabled, as the user-mode case below covers that configuration.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Spawn the worker threads in supervisor mode.\n- In each thread, write to the thread-local variables, yield to let the others run, and read them back.\n- Collect the per-thread results once every thread has finished.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Every thread reads back only the values it wrote itself.\n\n*See also:* Z_THREAD_LOCAL",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "thread_tls__test_tls_vars_are_per_thread"
        }
      ],
      "name": "thread_tls__test_tls_vars_are_per_thread",
      "summary": "Verify that thread-local variables are private to each thread."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/thread-tls--test-tls-vars-are-per-thread-user",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "User threads reach their thread-local storage through the same mechanism as supervisor threads but under memory protection, where the TLS block has to be mapped into the thread's own domain. The same isolation check is therefore repeated with the workers spawned as user threads, so a TLS block that is shared or unreachable under userspace is caught.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Spawn the worker threads with K_USER, inheriting the caller's permissions.\n- In each thread, write to the thread-local variables, yield to let the others run, and read them back.\n- Collect the per-thread results once every thread has finished.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Every user thread reads back only the values it wrote itself.\n\n*See also:* Z_THREAD_LOCAL\n\n*See also:* k_thread_create()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "thread_tls__test_tls_vars_are_per_thread_user"
        }
      ],
      "name": "thread_tls__test_tls_vars_are_per_thread_user",
      "summary": "Verify that thread-local variables stay private in user mode."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-abort-from-isr",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "k_thread_abort() is callable from interrupt context, including on the very thread the ISR interrupted. The kernel has to let the ISR run to completion and only then drop the aborted thread, rather than switching away inside the handler. The ISR sets a flag as its last act, so the test can tell that it finished as well as that the thread died.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Spawn a thread that enters an ISR and aborts itself from there.\n- Join the spawned thread.\n- Check the flag the ISR sets before returning.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The ISR completes and the interrupted thread is terminated.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_abort()\n\n*See also:* irq_offload()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_abort_from_isr"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_abort_from_isr",
      "summary": "Verify that a thread can be aborted from an ISR interrupting it."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-abort-from-isr-not-self",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The counterpart of aborting the interrupted thread: here the ISR runs on the test thread and aborts a different, already running thread. That target may be executing on another CPU or merely be ready, so the abort has to complete from interrupt context without waiting on the target to reach a scheduling point.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Spawn a thread and let it start running.\n- From the test thread, enter an ISR and abort the spawned thread there.\n- Join the spawned thread and check the flag the ISR sets.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The ISR completes and the target thread is terminated.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_abort()\n\n*See also:* irq_offload()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_abort_from_isr_not_self"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_abort_from_isr_not_self",
      "summary": "Verify that a thread can be aborted from an ISR on another thread."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-abort-others",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Aborting another thread has to work both before that thread has had a chance to run and after it has started, since the two take different paths: one removes a ready thread that was never scheduled, the other terminates one that is already executing. Both are covered here from user mode.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Create a user thread and abort it immediately, before it can run.\n- Sleep past the point it would have run and check its flag.\n- Create a second user thread, let it start, then abort it.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The thread aborted before starting never runs, and the one aborted after starting is terminated.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_abort()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_abort_others"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_abort_others",
      "summary": "Verify that a thread can be aborted by another thread."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-abort-self",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A thread calling k_thread_abort() on itself must not return from the call: it has to terminate at that point. The spawned thread raises a flag, aborts itself, and would raise a second value on the line after the call, so the flag distinguishes \"ran and stopped there\" from \"ran past the abort\".\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Create a user thread that flags that it ran and then aborts itself.\n- Sleep long enough for it to run.\n- Read the flag back.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The thread ran and stopped inside the abort, never reaching the code after it.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_abort()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_abort_self"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_abort_self",
      "summary": "Verify that a thread can abort itself."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-busy-wait",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "k_busy_wait() spins on the CPU rather than blocking, so the time it takes must show up as the calling thread's own execution cycles. Sampling the thread's runtime statistics on both sides of a busy wait of known length is what ties the accounting to real elapsed time.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Read the current thread's runtime statistics.\n- Call k_busy_wait() for a known duration.\n- Read the statistics again and compare the difference.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The thread's execution cycles grow by at least the time spent busy waiting.\n\n*See also:* k_busy_wait()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_runtime_stats_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_busy_wait"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_busy_wait",
      "summary": "Verify that busy waiting is accounted as execution time."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-current-get",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "k_current_get() must return the thread id of the caller: the id is stable across repeated calls within one thread, a spawned child observes its own (different) id from inside its entry function, and the parent still reads its own id after the child has run.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Read k_current_get() twice in the current thread and compare.\n- Spawn a child, passing the child's thread id as its argument.\n- The child asserts k_current_get() equals that id.\n- Join the child and verify the parent's id is unchanged and different from the child's.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Each thread sees its own thread id from k_current_get().\n\n*See also:* k_current_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_current_get"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_current_get",
      "summary": "Verify k_current_get() identifies the currently executing thread."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-essential-abort-panics",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "An essential thread is one the system cannot continue without, so the kernel must treat its termination as a fatal system error rather than reclaiming it quietly. The test installs a fatal error handler that records the event and lets the system continue, so the abort can be observed instead of halting the run.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Create a thread with the K_ESSENTIAL option and let it start.\n- Abort it from the test thread.\n- Check the flag recorded by the fatal error handler.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The kernel raises a fatal error for the aborted essential thread.\n\n*See also:* K_ESSENTIAL\n\n*See also:* k_thread_abort()\n\n*See also:* k_sys_fatal_error_handler()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_essential_abort_panics"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_essential_abort_panics",
      "summary": "Verify that aborting an essential thread raises a fatal error."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-essential-abort-self-panics",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The same rule has to hold when the essential thread ends itself rather than being aborted by someone else, which is the harder path: the panic is raised on the very thread that is going away, so the architecture layer has to unwind a thread that aborts inside its own fatal error handling.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Create a thread with the K_ESSENTIAL option whose entry point returns, ending the thread from within itself.\n- Wait for the fatal error handler to record the event.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The kernel raises a fatal error for the self-terminating essential thread.\n\n*See also:* K_ESSENTIAL\n\n*See also:* k_sys_fatal_error_handler()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_essential_abort_self_panics"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_essential_abort_self_panics",
      "summary": "Verify that an essential thread aborting itself raises a fatal error."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-essential-set-clear",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The essential flag is part of a thread's own state and can be turned on and off at run time, not only at creation. The spawned thread sets the flag, checks that it reads back as set, clears it and checks again, so what is validated is the flag the kernel actually recorded. Clearing it also lets the thread be aborted afterwards without panicking the kernel.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Create a thread that calls k_thread_essential_set() and confirms k_is_essential() reports it.\n- Have the thread clear the flag and confirm it no longer reports.\n- Signal the test thread, which then aborts the worker.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The flag reads back as set after being set and clear after being cleared, and the thread aborts without raising a fatal error.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_essential_set()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_essential_clear()\n\n*See also:* k_is_essential()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_essential_set_clear"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_essential_set_clear",
      "summary": "Verify that a thread can mark itself essential and clear it again."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-idle-priority",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The idle thread must be the lowest priority thread in the system, so that it only ever runs when nothing else can. Rather than inspecting the idle thread directly, the test sleeps to let it run and then checks that its own priority is still above K_IDLE_PRIO.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Sleep so the idle thread gets a chance to run on each CPU.\n- Compare the current thread's priority against K_IDLE_PRIO.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The running thread's priority is higher than the idle priority.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_priority_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_idle_priority"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_idle_priority",
      "summary": "Verify that the idle thread runs below every application thread."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-join",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A join has to return only once the target thread is really finished, whichever way it got there, and it has to behave the same when the thread has already ended before the join is issued. Each way a thread can reach its end is driven in turn so no single path can hide a join that returns early.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Join a thread that exits on its own.\n- Join a thread that is aborted while running.\n- Join a thread that is still waiting out a start delay.\n- Join a thread that had already exited before the join.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Every join returns success only after the target thread has ended.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_join()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_join"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_join",
      "summary": "Verify that k_thread_join() waits for a thread in every end state."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-join-deadlock",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A thread joining itself would wait forever for its own exit, so the kernel has to detect that and refuse rather than hang. Joins that only look circular must still be allowed, which is why two threads that join each other in a legal order are exercised alongside the self-join.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Call k_thread_join() on the calling thread itself.\n- Create two threads that join one another and join them from the test.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The self-join is rejected with -EDEADLK, and the two-thread joins all succeed.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_join()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_join_deadlock"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_join_deadlock",
      "summary": "Verify that k_thread_join() refuses to deadlock."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-join-isr",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Interrupt context must never block, so a join issued from an ISR can only be attempted without waiting. Joining a still-running thread has to be refused with -EBUSY, while joining one that has already finished can be satisfied immediately.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Spawn a thread and, from an ISR, join it with K_NO_WAIT while it runs.\n- Let the thread finish, then join it again from an ISR.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The join of the running thread returns -EBUSY, and the join of the finished thread succeeds.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_join()\n\n*See also:* irq_offload()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_join_isr"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_join_isr",
      "summary": "Verify that k_thread_join() from an ISR cannot block."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-main-priority",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The thread that runs main() has to be started at the priority named by CONFIG_MAIN_THREAD_PRIORITY, since applications rely on it to sit at a known point in the scheduling order. The priority is sampled during suite setup, before any case has had a chance to change it.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Record the main thread's priority in the suite setup function.\n- Compare it against CONFIG_MAIN_THREAD_PRIORITY.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The main thread runs at the configured priority.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_priority_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_main_priority"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_main_priority",
      "summary": "Verify that the main thread runs at its configured priority."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-name-get-set",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A thread's name is what identifies it in logs and debug output, so setting it has to be reflected by both accessors: k_thread_name_get(), which returns the kernel's own string, and k_thread_name_copy(), which copies it into a caller buffer. Too small a buffer must be reported rather than truncated silently.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Set a name on the current thread and on a spawned thread.\n- Read them back with k_thread_name_get() and compare.\n- Copy them out with k_thread_name_copy() and compare.\n- Attempt a copy into a buffer that is too small.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Both accessors report the name that was set, and the undersized copy fails rather than truncating.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_name_set()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_name_get()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_name_copy()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_name_get_set"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_name_get_set",
      "summary": "Verify that a thread name can be set and read back."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-name-user-get-set",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Reached from user mode, the name calls become system calls that must validate every pointer they are handed instead of trusting the caller: an unreadable source string, a buffer the caller does not own, and a thread object it has no permission for all have to be rejected rather than faulting the kernel. Skipped when userspace is not enabled.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Set and read back the current thread's name from user mode.\n- Attempt to set a name from a string the thread cannot read.\n- Attempt to copy a name into a buffer the thread does not own.\n- Attempt to name a thread the caller has no access to.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The legitimate calls succeed and every invalid pointer or permission is rejected with an error instead of a fault.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_name_set()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_name_copy()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_name_user_get_set"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_name_user_get_set",
      "summary": "Verify that thread names are handled safely from user mode."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-priority-set",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "k_thread_priority_set() must take effect on both the calling thread and another thread, and the new value has to be what k_thread_priority_get() subsequently reports. The second thread reads its own priority back and hands the result to the test through a semaphore, so what is checked is the priority the scheduler actually recorded rather than the value that was requested.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Lower the current thread's own priority and read it back.\n- Create a second thread and change its priority from the test thread.\n- Have the second thread read its own priority and report it.\n- Compare the reported priority against the requested one and join.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Both threads report exactly the priorities that were set for them.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_priority_set()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_priority_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_priority_set"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_priority_set",
      "summary": "Verify that a thread's priority can be changed and read back."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-priority-set-from-isr",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "k_thread_priority_set() is callable from interrupt context, where it must apply the same change without needing to reschedule inside the ISR. The same priority changes as the thread-context case are therefore driven through an offloaded interrupt, and the results are read back afterwards from thread context.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Lower the current thread's own priority from an ISR via irq_offload().\n- Read the priority back and compare it with the requested value.\n- Change a second thread's priority from an ISR the same way.\n- Have the second thread report its own priority and compare.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Both priority changes made from interrupt context take effect and are reported back.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_priority_set()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_priority_get()\n\n*See also:* irq_offload()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_priority_set_from_isr"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_priority_set_from_isr",
      "summary": "Verify that a thread's priority can be changed from an ISR."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-resume-not-suspended",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "k_thread_resume() on a thread that was never suspended has to be a no-op rather than disturbing whatever the thread is currently doing. The thread's state string is sampled before and after the call, so an unwanted change of state is visible rather than merely assumed absent.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Create a thread and let it reach a known state.\n- Read its state with k_thread_state_str().\n- Call k_thread_resume() on it, which was never suspended.\n- Read the state again and compare.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The thread state is unchanged by the resume.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_resume()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_state_str()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_resume_not_suspended"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_resume_not_suspended",
      "summary": "Verify that resuming a thread that is not suspended does nothing."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-runtime-stats-get",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The kernel accounts execution time both per thread and system wide, and the two views have to describe the same time: no thread's cycles may be missing from the total. Summing every thread's execution cycles and comparing against the global figure is what makes a dropped or double-counted thread visible.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Walk every thread and accumulate its execution cycles.\n- Read the system-wide runtime statistics.\n- Compare the accumulated sum against the reported total.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Every thread's execution time is reflected in the system total.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_runtime_stats_get()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_runtime_stats_all_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_runtime_stats_get"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_runtime_stats_get",
      "summary": "Verify that per-thread runtime statistics add up to the system total."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-spawn-delay",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A thread created with a start delay must not run before that delay has elapsed. The check is made at a point in time deliberately shorter than the delay, so a thread that started early is caught rather than merely being late.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Set a sentinel value that the thread would overwrite.\n- Create a user thread with a 120 ms start delay.\n- Sleep only 100 ms and read the sentinel back.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The sentinel is untouched, so the thread had not run yet.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_create()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_spawn_delay"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_spawn_delay",
      "summary": "Verify that a spawn delay defers the thread's first execution."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-spawn-forever",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A thread created with K_FOREVER is never made ready by the passage of time, only by an explicit k_thread_start(). It is created at the highest priority so that, once started, it runs immediately: that makes the difference between \"not yet started\" and \"started\" unambiguous even after the creator yields.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Create a user thread with K_FOREVER at the highest priority.\n- Yield, then confirm the thread has still not run.\n- Call k_thread_start() and check again.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Yielding does not run the thread; k_thread_start() does.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_create()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_start()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_spawn_forever"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_spawn_forever",
      "summary": "Verify that a K_FOREVER thread stays inactive until started."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-spawn-params",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The three parameters handed to k_thread_create() have to arrive unchanged at the thread's entry point. The spawned thread compares them itself and signals through a semaphore it was passed as one of them, so a pass proves both that the values survived and that a passed kernel object is usable.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Create a user thread, passing two values and a semaphore.\n- In the thread, compare the three parameters against what was passed.\n- Sleep long enough for the thread to run.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The thread observes exactly the parameters that were passed to it.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_create()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_spawn_params"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_spawn_params",
      "summary": "Verify that a spawned thread receives the parameters it was given."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-spawn-priority",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Creating a thread at a priority above the caller's must make it runnable at once and preempt the caller, rather than waiting for the caller to block. The spawned thread records the priority it observes so the placement can be checked as well as the fact that it ran.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Compute a priority one step higher than the current thread's.\n- Create a user thread at that priority.\n- Sleep, then check what the thread recorded.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The thread runs and reports the priority it was created with.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_create()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_priority_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_spawn_priority"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_spawn_priority",
      "summary": "Verify that a thread spawned at a higher priority preempts its creator."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-stack-space-get-user",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "k_thread_stack_space_get() reports how much of a thread's stack has never been touched, so the figure has to fall as the thread uses more of it. Calling it from a function with a large local variable and again from one with a small local gives two points whose order is known in advance, regardless of the absolute numbers, which vary by architecture.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Query the unused stack space from a function with a small local variable.\n- Query it again from a function with a large local variable.\n- Compare the two figures.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The deeper call reports less unused stack space than the shallow one.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_stack_space_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_stack_space_get_user"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_stack_space_get_user",
      "summary": "Verify that reported unused stack space tracks actual stack use."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-start",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "k_thread_start() on a thread that has already run to completion must be ignored rather than resurrecting it. The thread writes a value when it runs, so the test can reset that value after the thread has finished and see whether a second start would run the body again.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Create a K_FOREVER thread and start it, letting it run to completion.\n- Reset the value its body writes.\n- Call k_thread_start() on the terminated thread again.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The second start does nothing, so the value stays as it was reset.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_start()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_start"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_start",
      "summary": "Verify that starting an already terminated thread has no effect."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-start-user",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "k_thread_start() is reachable from user mode through its system call, so a user thread must be able to start a thread it was granted, and that thread must then actually run. The started thread writes a value its creator reads back.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- From a user thread, create a thread with a K_FOREVER start delay.\n- Call k_thread_start() on it.\n- Sleep, then read back the value the thread writes.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The started thread runs and writes its value.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_start()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_create()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_start_user"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_start_user",
      "summary": "Verify that a user thread can start an inactive thread."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-suspend",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "k_thread_suspend() called on the current thread has to act as a scheduling point: the thread must give up the CPU inside the call rather than running on to the next statement. The worker sets a flag on the line after the call, so that flag being clear is what proves the switch happened there.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Create a user thread that suspends itself and then sets a flag.\n- Give it time to start and run into the suspend.\n- Check the flag, then resume the thread and check it again.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The flag stays clear while the thread is suspended and is set once it is resumed, so the suspend switched away before the following statement.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_suspend()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_suspend"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_suspend",
      "summary": "Verify that a thread suspending itself reschedules immediately."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-suspend-resume-preempt",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The preemptible counterpart of the cooperative case, run from user mode so suspend and resume are reached through their system calls. A preemptible thread is eligible to be scheduled as soon as it is ready, which makes \"did not run while suspended\" the meaningful part of the check.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Create a preemptible thread and suspend it before it can run.\n- Sleep long enough that it would have run, and confirm it did not.\n- Resume it and sleep again.\n- Check that the entry function executed and that the thread kept the priority it was created with.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The thread does not run while suspended and does run once resumed.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_suspend()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_resume()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_suspend_resume_preempt"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_suspend_resume_preempt",
      "summary": "Verify that suspend and resume work on a preemptible thread."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-suspend-timeout",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A thread that is suspended while it happens to be sleeping must not be woken by the timeout it had pending. If the timeout survived the suspend, the thread would come back on its own, which is exactly what a suspended thread must never do.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Create a user thread that sleeps and then sets a flag on waking.\n- Suspend it part-way through its sleep.\n- Wait well past the point its sleep would have expired.\n- Check the flag.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The thread does not wake up, so the flag stays clear.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_suspend()\n\n*See also:* k_sleep()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_suspend_timeout"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_suspend_timeout",
      "summary": "Verify that suspending a sleeping thread cancels its timeout."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-timeout-remaining-expires",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A thread waiting out its start delay has a timeout pending, and the kernel can report it two ways: as the absolute tick the thread becomes ready, and as the ticks still to go. The absolute view must not fall before the deadline computed at creation, and the relative view has to keep shrinking as time passes, which is what sampling it repeatedly checks.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Compute the expected expiry tick and create a thread with that start delay.\n- Read the thread's expiry tick and compare it against the expected one.\n- Read its remaining ticks and confirm they are below the full delay.\n- Sleep and read the remaining ticks again.\n- Abort the thread before it starts.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The expiry tick is no earlier than the deadline set at creation, and each reading of the remaining ticks is smaller than the one before.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_timeout_remaining_ticks()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_timeout_expires_ticks()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_timeout_remaining_expires"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_timeout_remaining_expires",
      "summary": "Verify that a thread's pending timeout can be queried."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle--test-thread-user-mode",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "k_thread_user_mode_enter() converts the calling supervisor thread into an unprivileged one and does not return. The thread checks k_is_user_context() on the far side of the transition, so a pass proves the privilege level really changed rather than that the call merely returned.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Confirm the test thread is already running in user mode.\n- Create a supervisor thread that calls k_thread_user_mode_enter().\n- In its user-mode entry point, check k_is_user_context() and signal.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The thread continues in user mode and reports unprivileged context.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_user_mode_enter()\n\n*See also:* k_is_user_context()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_user_mode"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle__test_thread_user_mode",
      "summary": "Verify that a thread can drop into user mode."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle-1cpu--test-thread-abort-delayed",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A thread that is still waiting out its start delay is not yet on any ready queue, so aborting it has to cancel the pending start rather than leave a timeout that would launch a dead thread later. Sleeping past the original deadline is what makes a surviving timeout visible.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Create a thread with a 100 ms start delay.\n- Abort it while it is still waiting to start.\n- Sleep past the original start deadline.\n- Check the flag the thread would have set had it run.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The thread never runs, so its flag stays clear.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_abort()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle_1cpu__test_thread_abort_delayed"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle_1cpu__test_thread_abort_delayed",
      "summary": "Test abort on delayed thread before it has started execution."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle-1cpu--test-thread-abort-repeat",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A thread that has already been aborted may still be referenced by code that does not know it is gone, so a second k_thread_abort() on it has to be accepted quietly rather than corrupting the scheduler or faulting.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Create a thread and abort it.\n- Call k_thread_abort() on the same thread again, twice.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The repeated aborts complete without error and the system keeps running.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_abort()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle_1cpu__test_thread_abort_repeat"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle_1cpu__test_thread_abort_repeat",
      "summary": "Verify that aborting an already terminated thread is harmless."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle-1cpu--test-thread-busy-wait-user",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The same accounting has to hold when the busy wait is performed by a user thread, where both the wait and the statistics query are system calls. The case runs on a single CPU so the measurement is not disturbed by the thread migrating.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- From a user thread, read its runtime statistics.\n- Call k_busy_wait() for a known duration.\n- Read the statistics again and compare the difference.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The user thread's execution cycles grow by at least the time spent busy waiting.\n\n*See also:* k_busy_wait()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_runtime_stats_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle_1cpu__test_thread_busy_wait_user"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle_1cpu__test_thread_busy_wait_user",
      "summary": "Verify that busy waiting is accounted for a user thread."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle-1cpu--test-thread-cpu-mask",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The affinity APIs may only be applied to a thread that is prevented from running, and the resulting mask decides whether the scheduler may dispatch it at all. Both halves are checked: the calls are first refused on the running caller, then a not-yet-started high priority thread is given each shape of mask in turn and the test observes whether it runs when yielded to. Skipped unless CONFIG_SCHED_CPU_MASK is enabled.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Call each mask API on the running thread itself and check the return.\n- For each pass, create a higher priority thread in the K_FOREVER state and give it a mask: cleared, all enabled, CPU 0 disabled, or pinned to CPU 0.\n- Start the thread, yield, and record whether it ran.\n- Skip the pass that enables more than one CPU when PIN_ONLY is configured.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Every mask API returns -EINVAL for the running thread.\n- The thread runs only for the masks that leave it eligible for this CPU.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_cpu_mask_clear()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_cpu_mask_enable_all()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_cpu_mask_enable()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_cpu_mask_disable()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_cpu_pin()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle_1cpu__test_thread_cpu_mask"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle_1cpu__test_thread_cpu_mask",
      "summary": "Verify that a thread's CPU mask decides whether it can be scheduled."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle-1cpu--test-thread-custom-data-coop",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The custom data slot belongs to the thread that set it, so a value written by one thread must be readable by that same thread and must not be seen by another. The cooperative case writes and reads the slot repeatedly across yields, so a slot that is global rather than per thread shows up as a mismatch.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Set the custom data of the current thread and read it back.\n- Yield, then repeat several times with changing values.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Each read returns the value this thread most recently set.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_custom_data_set()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_custom_data_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle_1cpu__test_thread_custom_data_coop"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle_1cpu__test_thread_custom_data_coop",
      "summary": "Verify that custom data is per thread, from a cooperative thread."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle-1cpu--test-thread-custom-data-preempt",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The preemptible, user-mode counterpart of the cooperative custom data case. Here the accessors are reached as system calls and the thread can be preempted between the write and the read, so the slot has to survive arbitrary scheduling rather than only cooperative yields.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Set the custom data of the current user thread and read it back.\n- Yield, then repeat several times with changing values.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Each read returns the value this thread most recently set.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_custom_data_set()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_custom_data_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle_1cpu__test_thread_custom_data_preempt"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle_1cpu__test_thread_custom_data_preempt",
      "summary": "Verify that custom data is per thread, from a preemptible user thread."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle-1cpu--test-thread-foreach",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The iterator has to walk the kernel's whole thread list and invoke the caller's callback once per thread, picking up threads created since a previous walk. Counting the callbacks before and after creating a thread makes that growth observable. The iteration runs with the scheduler locked, so the callback must not create or abort threads.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Call k_thread_foreach() and record how many threads were visited.\n- Create an additional thread.\n- Call k_thread_foreach() again and compare the new count.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The first walk visits at least one thread, and the second visits exactly one more than the first.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_foreach()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle_1cpu__test_thread_foreach"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle_1cpu__test_thread_foreach",
      "summary": "Verify that k_thread_foreach() visits every thread, including new ones."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle-1cpu--test-thread-foreach-null-cb",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "There is nothing sensible to do with a NULL callback, so the iterator asserts on it rather than walking the thread list and dereferencing it. The assertion aborts this thread through the fatal error path, which the test harness expects.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Call k_thread_foreach() with a NULL callback.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The call raises the expected fatal error and does not return.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_foreach()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle_1cpu__test_thread_foreach_null_cb"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle_1cpu__test_thread_foreach_null_cb",
      "summary": "Verify that k_thread_foreach() rejects a NULL callback."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle-1cpu--test-thread-foreach-unlocked",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The unlocked iterator visits the same threads as the locked one but leaves the scheduler unlocked between callbacks, which is what makes it legal for the callback to create or abort threads. The callback used here aborts a thread while the walk is in progress, so a pass shows the iteration tolerates the list changing underneath it.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Call k_thread_foreach_unlocked() and record how many threads were visited.\n- Create an additional thread.\n- Call it again with a callback that aborts a thread mid-iteration.\n- Compare the counts.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The first walk visits at least one thread, the second visits exactly one more, and aborting from the callback does not disturb the walk.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_foreach_unlocked()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle_1cpu__test_thread_foreach_unlocked"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle_1cpu__test_thread_foreach_unlocked",
      "summary": "Verify that k_thread_foreach_unlocked() iterates without holding the scheduler lock."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle-1cpu--test-thread-foreach-unlocked-null-cb",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The unlocked iterator has to reject a NULL callback exactly as the locked one does, asserting instead of walking the thread list and dereferencing it.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Call k_thread_foreach_unlocked() with a NULL callback.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The call raises the expected fatal error and does not return.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_foreach_unlocked()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle_1cpu__test_thread_foreach_unlocked_null_cb"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle_1cpu__test_thread_foreach_unlocked_null_cb",
      "summary": "Verify that k_thread_foreach_unlocked() rejects a NULL callback."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle-1cpu--test-thread-state-str",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "k_thread_state_str() renders a thread's state bits as text for logs and shell output, so every state and combination it knows about has to produce the right string. Driving a real thread through each state in turn is not possible, so the state field of a spare thread object is set directly and the rendered string is compared against the expected name.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- For each thread state, write it into a spare thread object's state field.\n- Call k_thread_state_str() with a caller-provided buffer.\n- Compare the returned string with the expected text.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Every state, including combinations and the empty state, renders as its documented name.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_state_str()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle_1cpu__test_thread_state_str"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle_1cpu__test_thread_state_str",
      "summary": "Verify that k_thread_state_str() names every thread state."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-lifecycle-1cpu--test-thread-suspend-resume-coop",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A suspended thread must not be dispatched no matter what its priority would otherwise allow, and resuming it must make it runnable again. The worker is created at a cooperative priority and records that it ran, so the check is on observed execution rather than on a state flag.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Create a cooperative thread and suspend it before it can run.\n- Sleep long enough that it would have run, and confirm it did not.\n- Resume it and sleep again.\n- Check that the entry function executed and that the thread kept the priority it was created with.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The thread does not run while suspended and does run once resumed.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_suspend()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_resume()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_lifecycle_1cpu__test_thread_suspend_resume_coop"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_lifecycle_1cpu__test_thread_suspend_resume_coop",
      "summary": "Verify that suspend and resume work on a cooperative thread."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-scheduling--test-bad-priorities",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The kernel's priority-validity check must accept legal application priorities (and the idle priority only for the idle entry) and reject out-of-range values. The test runs a table of priority/entry combinations through both validity checks and confirms each expected accept/reject result.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Iterate a table of {priority, entry, expected-valid} cases.\n- For each, check the internal validity predicate and the Z_VALID_PRIO macro.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Every case returns its expected validity verdict.",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_scheduling__test_bad_priorities"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_scheduling__test_bad_priorities",
      "summary": "Verify thread priority validation accepts/rejects the right values."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-scheduling--test-busy-wait-cooperative",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "k_busy_wait() spins without yielding the CPU. From a cooperative thread (which is never preempted), no other ready thread may run for the whole busy-wait duration, unlike k_sleep() which would let them execute.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Set the current thread to a cooperative priority and spawn worker threads.\n- Call k_busy_wait() for 100 ms.\n- Verify none of the workers executed during the busy-wait.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- No other thread runs while the cooperative thread busy-waits.\n\n*See also:* k_busy_wait()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "threads_scheduling__test_busy_wait_cooperative"
        }
      ],
      "name": "threads_scheduling__test_busy_wait_cooperative",
      "summary": "Verify k_busy_wait() in a cooperative thread blocks other threads."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/threads-scheduling--test-k-thread-priority-get-init-null",
