    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-access-after-revoke",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "k_object_release() drops the calling thread's permission on an object. After releasing a semaphore it was granted, using that semaphore must oops exactly as if it had never been granted. The suite's fatal error handler compares the fault reason against the one the case armed and converts the expected fault into a pass; running past the access fails the case.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Release the granted semaphore with k_object_release().\n- Call k_sem_take() on it.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The take oopses with K_ERR_KERNEL_OOPS.\n\n*See also:* k_object_release()\n\n*See also:* k_sem_take()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_access_after_revoke"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_access_after_revoke",
      "summary": "Verify that a thread's own revocation of an object is enforced."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-bad-syscall",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "When a system call handler decides to terminate the calling thread, the kernel will produce error which indicates the context, where the faulting system call was made from user code.",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_bad_syscall"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_bad_syscall",
      "summary": "Test bad syscall handler."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-disable-mmu-mpu",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Turning off memory protection from user mode would defeat every other guarantee in this suite, so the attempt itself must trap, whatever form it takes on the architecture: clearing enable bits, rewriting protection entries or remapping regions. The suite's fatal error handler compares the fault reason against the one the case armed and converts the expected fault into a pass; running past the access fails the case.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Attempt the architecture's MMU/MPU disable sequence from user mode.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The attempt faults with K_ERR_CPU_EXCEPTION; the code after it is never reached.",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_disable_mmu_mpu"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_disable_mmu_mpu",
      "summary": "Verify that user mode cannot disable the MMU or MPU."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-is-post-kernel",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The predicate reads kernel state, so calling it from an unprivileged thread must neither fault nor claim the kernel is still initializing.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- From the user-mode test thread, call k_is_pre_kernel().\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The call succeeds and returns false.\n\n*See also:* k_is_pre_kernel()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_is_post_kernel"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_is_post_kernel",
      "summary": "Verify that k_is_pre_kernel() is callable and false in user mode."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-is-usermode",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Every negative case in this suite relies on the test thread being unprivileged, so this case anchors that assumption by asking the kernel directly.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- From the test thread, call k_is_user_context().\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The thread reports user context.\n\n*See also:* k_is_user_context()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_is_usermode"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_is_usermode",
      "summary": "Verify that a ZTEST_USER case really runs unprivileged."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-kernel-only-thread",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A thread defined with K_KERNEL_THREAD_DEFINE has no privilege-drop setup, so attempting k_thread_user_mode_enter() from it must raise a fatal error (panic, or oops on architectures that report it so) instead of reaching user mode.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Release the statically defined kernel-only thread.\n- Have it attempt k_thread_user_mode_enter().\n- Check which parts of it ran.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The kernel-mode half runs, the fatal error fires, and the user-mode entry point never executes.\n\n*See also:* K_KERNEL_THREAD_DEFINE\n\n*See also:* k_thread_user_mode_enter()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_kernel_only_thread"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_kernel_only_thread",
      "summary": "Verify that a K_KERNEL_THREAD-defined thread cannot enter user mode."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-object-recycle",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Test recycle valid/invalid kernel object, see if perms_count changes as expected.\n\n*See also:* k_object_recycle()\n\n*See also:* k_object_find()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_object_recycle"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_object_recycle",
      "summary": "Test recycle object."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-oops-exception",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "User code must not be able to forge fault reasons reserved for the kernel: requesting K_ERR_CPU_EXCEPTION arrives as an ordinary oops.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Invoke z_except_reason() with K_ERR_CPU_EXCEPTION from user mode.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The fault arrives as K_ERR_KERNEL_OOPS.",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_oops_exception"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_oops_exception",
      "summary": "Verify that a user-raised CPU-exception reason is downgraded to oops."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-oops-maxint",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A nonsense reason value from user mode must not reach the fatal error handler unfiltered.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Invoke z_except_reason() with INT_MAX from user mode.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The fault arrives as K_ERR_KERNEL_OOPS.",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_oops_maxint"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_oops_maxint",
      "summary": "Verify that an out-of-range oops reason is downgraded to oops."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-oops-oops",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The straightforward case: a user-raised oops arrives with its own reason.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Invoke z_except_reason() with K_ERR_KERNEL_OOPS from user mode.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The fault arrives as K_ERR_KERNEL_OOPS.\n\n*See also:* k_oops()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_oops_oops"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_oops_oops",
      "summary": "Verify that k_oops() from user mode is delivered as an oops."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-oops-panic",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A user thread must not be able to force a kernel panic: the panic reason is downgraded so only the offending thread dies. The suite's fatal error handler checks the delivered reason.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Invoke z_except_reason() with K_ERR_KERNEL_PANIC from user mode.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The fault arrives as K_ERR_KERNEL_OOPS.\n\n*See also:* k_panic()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_oops_panic"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_oops_panic",
      "summary": "Verify that k_panic() from user mode lands as an oops."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-oops-stackcheck",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "K_ERR_STACK_CHK_FAIL is a legitimate reason for user code to raise, so unlike the forged kernel reasons it must be delivered unchanged.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Invoke z_except_reason() with K_ERR_STACK_CHK_FAIL from user mode.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The fault arrives as K_ERR_STACK_CHK_FAIL.",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_oops_stackcheck"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_oops_stackcheck",
      "summary": "Verify that a user-raised stack-check failure keeps its reason."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-pass-noperms-object",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A genuine kernel object the calling thread was never granted must be refused the same way as a fake one. The suite's fatal error handler compares the fault reason against the one the case armed and converts the expected fault into a pass; running past the access fails the case.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Call k_sem_init() on a kernel semaphore never granted to the thread.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The call oopses with K_ERR_KERNEL_OOPS.\n\n*See also:* k_sem_init()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_pass_noperms_object"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_pass_noperms_object",
      "summary": "Verify that a system call rejects an object the caller lacks permission on."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-read-kernel-data",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A file-scope kernel variable is not in any partition granted to the test thread, so even a read of it must trap. The suite's fatal error handler compares the fault reason against the one the case armed and converts the expected fault into a pass; running past the access fails the case.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Read a kernel static variable from user mode.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The read faults with K_ERR_CPU_EXCEPTION.",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_read_kernel_data"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_read_kernel_data",
      "summary": "Verify that user mode cannot read a kernel static variable."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-read-kernram",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The kernel's own data here a field of the current thread's kernel structure must be unreadable from user mode. The suite's fatal error handler compares the fault reason against the one the case armed and converts the expected fault into a pass; running past the access fails the case.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Read a member of the current thread's kernel object from user mode.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The read faults with K_ERR_CPU_EXCEPTION.",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_read_kernram"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_read_kernram",
      "summary": "Verify that user mode cannot read kernel RAM."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-read-kobject-user-pipe",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "k_pipe_write() reads from a caller-supplied buffer, so pointing it at a kernel object would exfiltrate kernel memory through the pipe. The read-validation of the syscall boundary has to refuse it. The suite's fatal error handler compares the fault reason against the one the case armed and converts the expected fault into a pass; running past the access fails the case.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Call k_pipe_write() with a kernel semaphore's address as the buffer.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The call oopses with K_ERR_KERNEL_OOPS.\n\n*See also:* k_pipe_write()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_read_kobject_user_pipe"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_read_kobject_user_pipe",
      "summary": "Verify that a syscall input buffer cannot leak a kernel object."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-read-other-stack",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "With CONFIG_MEM_DOMAIN_ISOLATED_STACKS the stacks of threads in the same memory domain are isolated from each other, so a spawned thread reading a stack variable of its parent must fault. Skipped where that isolation is not configured, since the baseline memory domain model permits the access. The suite's fatal error handler compares the fault reason against the one the case armed and converts the expected fault into a pass; running past the access fails the case.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Spawn a user thread and hand it the address of a parent stack variable.\n- Have it read through that pointer.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The read faults with K_ERR_CPU_EXCEPTION.",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_read_other_stack"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_read_other_stack",
      "summary": "Verify that one user thread cannot read another's stack."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-revoke-noperms-object",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "User thread can only revoke their own access to an object. In that test user thread to revokes access to unauthorized object, as a result the system will assert.",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_revoke_noperms_object"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_revoke_noperms_object",
      "summary": "Test to revoke access to kobject without permission."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-start-kernel-thread",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Creating a thread without K_USER from user mode would mint privileged execution out of unprivileged code, so the create call must oops. The suite's fatal error handler compares the fault reason against the one the case armed and converts the expected fault into a pass; running past the access fails the case.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Call k_thread_create() from user mode without the K_USER option.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The call oopses with K_ERR_KERNEL_OOPS.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_create()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_start_kernel_thread"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_start_kernel_thread",
      "summary": "Verify that user mode cannot create a supervisor thread."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-syscall-context",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "While handling a system call from user mode the kernel must run with interrupts unlocked and must not consider itself in ISR context; either would distort every syscall that checks or manipulates that state.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Invoke a test system call whose implementation checks the IRQ lock state and k_is_in_isr().\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Interrupts are unlocked and no ISR context is reported inside the call.\n\n*See also:* k_is_in_isr()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_syscall_context"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_syscall_context",
      "summary": "Verify the kernel context in which system calls execute."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-tls-leakage",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The thread's userspace-local-data area is written with a marker while still in supervisor mode; after dropping to user mode none of the marker must remain readable, while the area itself stays fully accessible to the user thread. Skipped without CONFIG_THREAD_USERSPACE_LOCAL_DATA.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Fill the thread's userspace local data with a marker in supervisor mode.\n- Drop to user mode and scan the area.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- No byte of the supervisor-written marker survives into user mode.\n\n*See also:* k_thread_user_mode_enter()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_tls_leakage"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_tls_leakage",
      "summary": "Verify that supervisor TLS contents do not leak into user mode."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-tls-pointer",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The userspace local data of a thread lives inside that thread's own stack allocation; a TLS pointer outside those bounds would alias other memory. Skipped without CONFIG_THREAD_USERSPACE_LOCAL_DATA.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Create a user thread without starting it.\n- Compare its userspace_local_data range against its stack object bounds.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The TLS area lies entirely within the thread's stack object.",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_tls_pointer"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_tls_pointer",
      "summary": "Verify that a user thread's TLS area lies within its stack object."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-unimplemented-syscall",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Created a syscall with name missing_syscall() without a verification function. The kernel shall safety handle invocations of unimplemented system calls.",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_unimplemented_syscall"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_unimplemented_syscall",
      "summary": "Test unimplemented system call."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-user-mode-enter",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A thread running in supervisor mode must have one-way operation ability to drop privileges to user mode.",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_user_mode_enter"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_user_mode_enter",
      "summary": "Test to check supervisor thread enter one-way to usermode."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-write-control",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Privileged CPU state control, status or protection registers, whichever the architecture exposes must be out of reach: an unprivileged write has to trap instead of taking effect. On Cortex-M the write is silently ignored, so the register is read back unchanged instead of expecting a fault. The suite's fatal error handler compares the fault reason against the one the case armed and converts the expected fault into a pass; running past the access fails the case.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Attempt the architecture's privileged register access from user mode.\n- On Cortex-M, read the register back instead.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The access faults with K_ERR_CPU_EXCEPTION (or is proven ineffective on Cortex-M).",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_write_control"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_write_control",
      "summary": "Verify that user mode cannot write CPU control registers."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-write-kernel-data",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The write counterpart of the kernel data read. The suite's fatal error handler compares the fault reason against the one the case armed and converts the expected fault into a pass; running past the access fails the case.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Write a kernel static variable from user mode.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The write faults with K_ERR_CPU_EXCEPTION.",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_write_kernel_data"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_write_kernel_data",
      "summary": "Verify that user mode cannot write a kernel static variable."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-write-kernram",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The write counterpart of the kernel RAM read: storing to a field of the current thread's kernel structure must trap. The suite's fatal error handler compares the fault reason against the one the case armed and converts the expected fault into a pass; running past the access fails the case.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Write a member of the current thread's kernel object from user mode.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The write faults with K_ERR_CPU_EXCEPTION.",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_write_kernram"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_write_kernram",
      "summary": "Verify that user mode cannot write kernel RAM."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-write-kernro",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A kernel variable placed in .rodata must reject user-mode writes. The case first proves the chosen symbol really lives in the rodata region, so the fault genuinely exercises read-only protection. The suite's fatal error handler compares the fault reason against the one the case armed and converts the expected fault into a pass; running past the access fails the case.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Check the target symbol's address lies inside the rodata region.\n- Write to it from user mode.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The write faults with K_ERR_CPU_EXCEPTION.",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_write_kernro"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_write_kernro",
      "summary": "Verify that user mode cannot write the kernel's read-only data."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-write-kerntext",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Patching kernel text from user mode is the classic privilege escalation, so overwriting the body of a kernel function must trap. The suite's fatal error handler compares the fault reason against the one the case armed and converts the expected fault into a pass; running past the access fails the case.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- memset() over a kernel function from user mode.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The write faults with K_ERR_CPU_EXCEPTION.",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_write_kerntext"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_write_kerntext",
      "summary": "Verify that user mode cannot write kernel code."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-write-kobject-user-pipe",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "k_pipe_read() writes into a caller-supplied buffer, so pointing that buffer at a kernel object would let the kernel overwrite its own state on the user's behalf. The write-validation of the syscall boundary has to refuse it. The suite's fatal error handler compares the fault reason against the one the case armed and converts the expected fault into a pass; running past the access fails the case.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Call k_pipe_read() with a kernel semaphore's address as the buffer.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The call oopses with K_ERR_KERNEL_OOPS.\n\n*See also:* k_pipe_read()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_write_kobject_user_pipe"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_write_kobject_user_pipe",
      "summary": "Verify that a syscall output buffer cannot overlay a kernel object."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-write-other-stack",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The write counterpart of the cross-stack read, under the same CONFIG_MEM_DOMAIN_ISOLATED_STACKS condition. The suite's fatal error handler compares the fault reason against the one the case armed and converts the expected fault into a pass; running past the access fails the case.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Spawn a user thread and hand it the address of a parent stack variable.\n- Have it write through that pointer.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The write faults with K_ERR_CPU_EXCEPTION.",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_write_other_stack"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_write_other_stack",
      "summary": "Verify that one user thread cannot write another's stack."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace--test-userspace-write-priv-stack",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The write counterpart of the privileged stack read: corrupting the stack the kernel will use for this thread's next system call must be impossible. The suite's fatal error handler compares the fault reason against the one the case armed and converts the expected fault into a pass; running past the access fails the case.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Locate the current thread's privileged stack.\n- Write one byte of it from user mode.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The write faults with K_ERR_CPU_EXCEPTION.",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace__test_userspace_write_priv_stack"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace__test_userspace_write_priv_stack",
      "summary": "Verify that user mode cannot write its own privileged stack."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace-access-check--test-kobject-access-check",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The check call lets a user thread ask, without side effects, whether it may use an object. Each answer must match the actual state: granted object, object never granted, address that is no kernel object, and the thread's own thread object, which every thread implicitly owns.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Check a semaphore granted during suite setup.\n- Check a semaphore that was never granted.\n- Check an address that is not a kernel object.\n- Check the calling thread's own thread object.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The calls return 0, -EPERM, -EBADF and 0 respectively.\n\n*See also:* k_object_access_check()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace_access_check__test_kobject_access_check"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace_access_check__test_kobject_access_check",
      "summary": "Verify that k_object_access_check() reports the caller's rights."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace-domain--test-userspace-1st-init-and-access-other-memdomain",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A freshly initialized domain containing \"alt_part\" but not \"default_part\" must deny access to the latter: a member thread writing data in \"default_part\" has to fault. This once caught an MMU port that copied the source page tables' partition permissions into the new domain, which made the forbidden write succeed. The \"1st\" in the name makes ztest's alphabetical ordering run this case first, as it initializes the alternate domain the other domain cases reuse. The suite's fatal error handler converts the expected fault into a pass.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Initialize the alternate domain with the alternate partition only.\n- Move the current thread into it and spawn a user thread that writes a variable in the default partition.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The write faults with K_ERR_CPU_EXCEPTION.\n\n*See also:* k_mem_domain_init()\n\n*See also:* k_mem_domain_add_thread()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace_domain__test_userspace_1st_init_and_access_other_memdomain"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace_domain__test_userspace_1st_init_and_access_other_memdomain",
      "summary": "Verify that a new memory domain grants only its own partitions."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace-domain--test-userspace-domain-add-part-drop-to-user",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Adding a partition to the default domain must grant its member threads access as soon as they run in user mode.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Add the alternate partition to the default domain.\n- Drop to user mode and write a variable in that partition.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The write succeeds with no fault.\n\n*See also:* k_mem_domain_add_partition()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_user_mode_enter()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace_domain__test_userspace_domain_add_part_drop_to_user"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace_domain__test_userspace_domain_add_part_drop_to_user",
      "summary": "Verify a partition added to a domain is visible after dropping to user."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace-domain--test-userspace-domain-add-thread-drop-to-user",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Moving the calling thread into the alternate domain and then entering user mode must apply that domain's partitions: the user half writes a variable in a partition only the alternate domain carries, which succeeds only if the switch took effect.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Add the current thread to the alternate domain.\n- Drop to user mode and write a variable in the alternate partition.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The write succeeds with no fault.\n\n*See also:* k_mem_domain_add_thread()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_user_mode_enter()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace_domain__test_userspace_domain_add_thread_drop_to_user"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace_domain__test_userspace_domain_add_thread_drop_to_user",
      "summary": "Verify domain reassignment applies when dropping to user mode."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace-domain--test-userspace-domain-remove-part-drop-to-user",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The inverse of the add case: after the partition added by the previous case is removed again, a user-mode access to it must fault. Ztest runs the suite alphabetically, so the add_part case has already run. The suite's fatal error handler converts the expected fault into a pass.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Remove the alternate partition from the default domain.\n- Drop to user mode and write a variable in that partition.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The write faults with K_ERR_CPU_EXCEPTION.\n\n*See also:* k_mem_domain_remove_partition()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace_domain__test_userspace_domain_remove_part_drop_to_user"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace_domain__test_userspace_domain_remove_part_drop_to_user",
      "summary": "Verify a partition removed from a domain is revoked on entering user."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace-domain-ctx--test-userspace-domain-add-part-context-switch",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The context-switch counterpart of the add_part drop-to-user case.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Add the alternate partition to the default domain.\n- Spawn a user thread that writes a variable in that partition and join it.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The spawned thread's write succeeds with no fault.\n\n*See also:* k_mem_domain_add_partition()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace_domain_ctx__test_userspace_domain_add_part_context_switch"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace_domain_ctx__test_userspace_domain_add_part_context_switch",
      "summary": "Verify a partition added to a domain is visible across a context switch."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace-domain-ctx--test-userspace-domain-add-thread-context-switch",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The same domain-move check as the drop-to-user variant, but observed from a freshly spawned user thread, so the domain configuration is applied on a context switch rather than a privilege drop.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Add the current thread to the alternate domain.\n- Spawn a user thread that writes a variable in the alternate partition and join it.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The spawned thread's write succeeds with no fault.\n\n*See also:* k_mem_domain_add_thread()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace_domain_ctx__test_userspace_domain_add_thread_context_switch"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace_domain_ctx__test_userspace_domain_add_thread_context_switch",
      "summary": "Verify domain reassignment applies across a context switch."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace-domain-ctx--test-userspace-domain-remove-part-context-switch",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The context-switch counterpart of the remove_part drop-to-user case; the partition added by the preceding add_part case is removed and the next user thread to touch it must fault. The suite's fatal error handler converts the expected fault into a pass.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Remove the alternate partition from the default domain.\n- Spawn a user thread that writes a variable in that partition.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- The spawned thread's write faults with K_ERR_CPU_EXCEPTION.\n\n*See also:* k_mem_domain_remove_partition()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace_domain_ctx__test_userspace_domain_remove_part_context_switch"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace_domain_ctx__test_userspace_domain_remove_part_context_switch",
      "summary": "Verify a removed partition is revoked across a context switch."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace-domain-switching--test-userspace-kernel-only-switching",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Threads pinned to one CPU alternate through the scheduler while belonging to two disjoint memory domains even-numbered threads in domain A, odd in domain B. Every reschedule must swap in the incoming thread's domain: each thread increments a counter in its own partition every loop, and the counter in the other domain's partition must stay untouched, so a stale domain after a switch shows up as a fault or a stray count. Skipped without CONFIG_USERSPACE_SWITCHING_TESTS.\n\nThis variant runs every thread as a kernel thread, which has access to all memory regardless of domains, proving the scenario itself is sound before the user-mode variants rely on it.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Run the switching scenario with all threads created as kernel threads.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Every thread completes its loops and the cross-partition counters stay zero.",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace_domain_switching__test_userspace_kernel_only_switching"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace_domain_switching__test_userspace_kernel_only_switching",
      "summary": "Verify the switching scenario with kernel threads as a baseline."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace-domain-switching--test-userspace-kernel-user-mix-switching",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Threads pinned to one CPU alternate through the scheduler while belonging to two disjoint memory domains even-numbered threads in domain A, odd in domain B. Every reschedule must swap in the incoming thread's domain: each thread increments a counter in its own partition every loop, and the counter in the other domain's partition must stay untouched, so a stale domain after a switch shows up as a fault or a stray count. Skipped without CONFIG_USERSPACE_SWITCHING_TESTS.\n\nOne thread stays a kernel thread while the rest run in user mode, so the scheduler alternates between threads that bypass the domains and threads confined by them the transition each direction must restore the right protection view.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Run the switching scenario with one kernel thread and the rest user threads.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Every thread completes its loops and the cross-partition counters stay zero.\n\n*See also:* k_mem_domain_add_thread()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace_domain_switching__test_userspace_kernel_user_mix_switching"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace_domain_switching__test_userspace_kernel_user_mix_switching",
      "summary": "Verify domain isolation with kernel and user threads interleaved."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace-domain-switching--test-userspace-user-only-switching",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Threads pinned to one CPU alternate through the scheduler while belonging to two disjoint memory domains even-numbered threads in domain A, odd in domain B. Every reschedule must swap in the incoming thread's domain: each thread increments a counter in its own partition every loop, and the counter in the other domain's partition must stay untouched, so a stale domain after a switch shows up as a fault or a stray count. Skipped without CONFIG_USERSPACE_SWITCHING_TESTS.\n\nThis is the full test: every thread is a user thread confined to its domain, so each context switch has to reprogram the protection hardware.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Run the switching scenario with all threads created as user threads, assigned alternately to the two domains.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Every thread completes its loops and the cross-partition counters stay zero.\n\n*See also:* k_mem_domain_add_thread()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace_domain_switching__test_userspace_user_only_switching"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace_domain_switching__test_userspace_user_only_switching",
      "summary": "Verify domain isolation while switching among user threads."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/userspace-thread-stack--test-thread-stack-buffer-access",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A thread must be able to read and write the whole stack buffer it was given, and the size and alignment reported by the K_THREAD_STACK and K_KERNEL_STACK macros have to agree with what the object actually occupies. Getting this wrong is what turns a stack into an overlapping or under-sized region, so each form a stack can be declared in is checked in turn: a single stack, an element of a stack array, and a stack embedded in a struct, for both thread and kernel stacks.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- Report the reserved space and interrupt stack sizes for the record.\n- For each declared stack, spawn a thread on it and have that thread check its reported bounds, alignment and reserved space, then write over the full usable extent of its own stack.\n- Repeat for user stacks, user stack arrays, kernel stacks, kernel stack arrays and a struct-embedded stack.\n\nExpected result:\n\n- Every stack reports the size and alignment its declaration implies, and a thread can write the whole buffer without faulting.\n\n*See also:* K_THREAD_STACK_DEFINE()\n\n*See also:* K_KERNEL_STACK_DEFINE()\n\n*See also:* K_THREAD_STACK_SIZEOF()\n\n*See also:* k_thread_stack_space_get()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "userspace_thread_stack__test_thread_stack_buffer_access"
        }
      ],
      "name": "userspace_thread_stack__test_thread_stack_buffer_access",
      "summary": "Verify that every kind of declared stack is sized, aligned and writable as its macros promise."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/work--test-drain-empty",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "*See also:* k_work_queue_drain()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
